ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/176/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,2473,3200/32429_Washul_Horizon_Talk_930.rev.1788967234.jp
 g
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:Dr. Eveline Washul will discuss her new book\, High Lands\, Pu
 re Earth: Place Making\, History\, and Urban Transitions on the Tibetan Pl
 ateau (Cornell 2026). In the twenty-first century\, China's accelerated ur
 banization has transformed the Tibetan Plateau. Yet Tibetan urban experien
 ces continue to be profoundly shaped by senses of being in the world that 
 long predate Tibet's subjugation into the modern Chinese nation-state. Com
 bining novel analysis of Tibetan textual sources\, ethnographic research\,
  and Indigenous studies frameworks\, Washul's book explores how Tibetans a
 ctively engage history\, peoplehood\, and place to build belonging and com
 munity even in urban spaces characterized by intense state-led development
  and assimilation.Please note that this event will be in person only. Ther
 e will not be a Zoom or hybrid option. Lunch will be provided.This event i
 s sponsored by:Department of Central Eurasian StudiesInner Asian and Urali
 c National Resource CenterDepartment of AnthropologyEast Asian Studies Cen
 terDepartment of East Asian Languages and CulturesNative American and Indi
 genous Studies Program
GEO:39.170002;-86.517492
LAST-MODIFIED:20260923T192452Z
LOCATION:GLOBAL &amp\; INTERNATIONAL STUDIES BUILDING
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2410825-aces-horizon-talk-book-talk-
 with-dr-eveline-washul
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Dr. Eveline Washul will discuss her new 
 book\, <i>High Lands\, Pure Earth: Place Making\, History\, and Urban Tran
 sitions on the Tibetan Plateau</i> (Cornell 2026). In the twenty-first cen
 tury\, China's accelerated urbanization has transformed the Tibetan Platea
 u. Yet Tibetan urban experiences continue to be profoundly shaped by sense
 s of being in the world that long predate Tibet's subjugation into the mod
 ern Chinese nation-state. Combining novel analysis of Tibetan textual sour
 ces\, ethnographic research\, and Indigenous studies frameworks\, Washul's
  book explores how Tibetans actively engage history\, peoplehood\, and pla
 ce to build belonging and community even in urban spaces characterized by 
 intense state-led development and assimilation.</p><p><strong>Please note 
 that this event will be in person only</strong><strong>. There will not be
  a Zoom or hybrid option. Lunch will be provided.</strong></p><p><em>This 
 event is sponsored by:</em></p><div><ul><li><em><span role="presentation" 
 data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody">Department of Central Eurasian Studies<
 /span></em></li></ul></div><div><ul><li><em><span role="presentation">Inne
