X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 135": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2382618-ostrom-workshop-research-series-anasthascia"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_135
SUMMARY:Ostrom Workshop | Research Series: Anasthascia Boateng
DTSTART:20260930T170000Z
DTEND:20260930T180000Z
DTSTAMP:20260923T152509Z
UID:20260930T170000Z-2428215@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20260930T170000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/105/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,298,276/12535_research_book_spin_image.rev.1659037749.png
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:Bio  Anasthascia Boateng is a fourth-year Ph.D. student in the
  Department of Anthropology. Her research examines how African internation
 al students in the United States navigate transnational care\, resource sh
 aring\, and family obligations within diasporic communities. Drawing on ec
 onomic anthropology\, migration studies\, and diaspora philanthropy\, she 
 examines the concept of "Black Tax\," the expectation that individuals who
  achieve educational or economic mobility provide support to family and co
 mmunity members across borders. As an Ostrom research awardee and fellow\,
  she investigates how informal institutions\, collective action\, and shar
 ed obligations shape students' everyday lives and contribute to broader co
 nversations about governance\, reciprocity\, and social support in transna
 tional settings.Title  "Black Tax": Diaspora Philanthropy or Burden? Gover
 ning Transnational Care among African International StudentsAbstract  This
  paper examines how African international students in the United States na
 vigate the financial\, emotional\, and social obligations associated with 
 Black tax the expectation that upwardly mobile individuals will provide ma
 terial and social support to family members and communities across borders
 . Drawing on in-depth interviews with my interlocutors\, the study investi
 gates whether remittance is experienced as diaspora philanthropy\, moral r
 esponsibility\, collective care\, or personal burden and under what condit
 ions these meanings shift. The findings reveal that remittances operate si
 multaneously as expressions of love\, kinship obligation\, and collective 
 investment\, while also requiring students to reduce personal consumption\
 , defer savings\, and make difficult trade-offs between transnational resp
 onsibilities and daily life in the United States.About the Ostrom Workshop
  Research Series  Learn about innovative research by Graduate Students and
  Visiting Scholars as they advance the mission of the Ostrom Workshop\, bu
 ilding upon the theme of governance to understand and address major societ
 al problems.
GEO:39.171057;-86.524959
LAST-MODIFIED:20260923T192509Z
LOCATION:The Ostrom Workshop\, 513 N PARK AVE
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2382618-ostrom-workshop-research-ser
 ies-anasthascia
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><picture class="lw_image lw_image31981  lw
 _align_left lw_block"><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://events.iu.
 edu/live/image/scale/2x/gid/105/width/300/height/300/crop/1/src_region/0\,
 94\,1320\,1413/31981_ABoateng.rev.1786724464.webp 2x\, https://events.iu.e
