 0" height="300" alt="" data-alt="" data-decoration="true" src="https://eve
 nts.iu.edu/live/image/gid/105/width/300/height/300/crop/1/src_region/0\,94
 \,1320\,1413/31981_ABoateng.rev.1786724464.jpg" srcset="https://events.iu.
 edu/live/image/scale/2x/gid/105/width/300/height/300/crop/1/src_region/0\,
 94\,1320\,1413/31981_ABoateng.rev.1786724464.jpg 2x\, https://events.iu.ed
 u/live/image/scale/3x/gid/105/width/300/height/300/crop/1/src_region/0\,94
 \,1320\,1413/31981_ABoateng.rev.1786724464.jpg 3x" data-max-w="1320" data-
 max-h="1319" loading="lazy" data-optimized="true"/></picture></p><p><stron
 g>Bio</strong></p><p>  Anasthascia Boateng is a fourth-year Ph.D. student 
 in the Department of Anthropology. Her research examines how African inter
 national students in the United States navigate transnational care\, resou
 rce sharing\, and family obligations within diasporic communities. Drawing
  on economic anthropology\, migration studies\, and diaspora philanthropy\
 , she examines the concept of "Black Tax\," the expectation that individua
 ls who achieve educational or economic mobility provide support to family 
 and community members across borders. As an Ostrom research awardee and fe
 llow\, she investigates how informal institutions\, collective action\, an
 d shared obligations shape students' everyday lives and contribute to broa
 der conversations about governance\, reciprocity\, and social support in t
 ransnational settings.</p><p><strong>Title</strong></p><p>  "Black Tax": D
 iaspora Philanthropy or Burden? Governing Transnational Care among African
  International Students</p><p><strong>Abstract</strong></p><p>  This paper
  examines how African international students in the United States navigate
  the financial\, emotional\, and social obligations associated with Black 
 tax the expectation that upwardly mobile individuals will provide material
  and social support to family members and communities across borders. Draw
 ing on in-depth interviews with my interlocutors\, the study investigates 
 whether remittance is experienced as diaspora philanthropy\, moral respons
 ibility\, collective care\, or personal burden and under what conditions t
 hese meanings shift. The findings reveal that remittances operate simultan
 eously as expressions of love\, kinship obligation\, and collective invest
 ment\, while also requiring students to reduce personal consumption\, defe
 r savings\, and make difficult trade-offs between transnational responsibi
 lities and daily life in the United States.</p><p><strong>About the Ostrom
  Workshop Research Series</strong></p><p>  Learn about innovative research
  by Graduate Students and Visiting Scholars as they advance the mission of
  the Ostrom Workshop\, building upon the theme of governance to understand
  and address major societal problems.</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<p>\n  Karen Mack\, Ostrom Workshop Marketing &am
 p\; Special Events Coordinator\, <a href="mailto:workshop@indiana.edu">wor
 kshop@indiana.edu</a>&#160\;\n</p>
