SUMMARY:CRRES Symposium: Venezuelan Politics in the Wake of Crisis
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261002
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261003
DTSTAMP:20260914T120751Z
UID:20261002T040000Z-2416925@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID;VALUE=DATE:20261002
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/137/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,1545,1479/32478_CRRES_Symposium_Venezuelan_Politics_in_th
 e_Wake_of_Crisis_UPDATE.rev.1789148514.png
CATEGORIES:Culture and languages,Lectures, talks, seminars, and conference
 s
DESCRIPTION:Venezuelan Politics in the Wake of CrisisFriday\, October 2nd9
 :30a.m.-4:00p.m.Breakfast served at 9amHamilton Lugar School -Shreve Audit
 oriumFor Schedule: Center for Research Race &amp\; Ethnicity in Society's 
 Event Page (https://crres.indiana.edu/events/venezuela-symposium.html)&#16
 0\;  Against the backdrop of political upheaval\, foreign intervention\, n
 atural disaster\, and long-standing infrastructure challenges\, Venezuelan
  Politics in the Wake of Crisis will bring together scholars and a human r
 ights advocate for an interdisciplinary examination of contemporary Venezu
 ela.  The symposium will feature Verónica Zubillaga of the University of 
 Illinois Chicago and Yoletty Bracho of Avignon University\, joining in per
 son\; Magdalena López of the University of Notre Dame and the University 
 Institute of Lisbon\, joining virtually\; and Venezuelan attorney and huma
 n rights advocate Venus Marina Faddoul Barrios\, director of the NGO 100% 
 Estrógeno\, joining virtually from Spain.  Drawing on expertise in politi
 cal participation\, urban and state violence\, cultural criticism\, human 
 rights\, and gender justice\, the panelists will examine how Venezuela's c
 ontinuing crises have affected political life\, civil society\, migration 
 and displacement\, and regional relations. The program will also include a
  screening of 22 de mayo (1969) by Cine Urgente moderated by Dr. Noraeden 
 Mora from the Department of Spanish and Portuguese.  Organized by the Cent
 er for Research on Race and Ethnicity in Society (CRRES)\, in collaboratio
 n with the Center for Latin American and Caribbean Studies (CLACS)\, the H
 amilton Lugar School of Global and International Studies (HLS)\, the Depar
 tment of History\, and the Department of Spanish and Portuguese.
LAST-MODIFIED:20260925T171944Z
LOCATION:Shreve Auditorium
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2415043-venezuelan-politics-in-the-w
 ake-of-crisis
