BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 64": "https://events.iu.edu/lawiub/event/2428181-such-now-the-necessity-the-declaration-and-secession"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_64
SUMMARY:Such Now the Necessity: The Declaration and Secession Symposium
DTSTART:20261002T140000Z
DTEND:20261002T190000Z
DTSTAMP:20260923T151407Z
UID:20261002T140000Z-2428181@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261002T140000Z
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:The symposium considers the Declaration specifically as a sece
 ssionist document: how it functioned in the early United States\, how it r
 elates to broader theories of secession\, and how it has been or might be 
 used in other political contexts.  The Declaration of Independence is reve
 red as the formative expression of American values. However\, it was desig
 ned primarily as a legal and rhetorical address to the world—"to the opi
 nions of mankind"—justifying a radical project: secession. The Declarati
 on advances the two principal arguments that are familiar justifications u
 sed by secession movements today: an inherent right to self-government bas
 ed on "consent of the governed\," and "remedial secession" in response to 
 "a long train of abuses." Given this founding DNA\, why is the United Stat
 es no longer a pro-secession advocate? What would it mean to take the Decl
 aration's arguments seriously today—both in America's foreign relations 
 and for America's own territories (such as Puerto Rico) and the 50 states?
   Schedule:  Panel 1. The Declaration and Secession in the early United St
 ates 10:30 to 12:00Kon Dierks\, Indiana University  Eric Slauter\, Univers
 ity of Chicago  Noah Eber-Schmidt\, Indiana University  Timothy William Wa
 ters\, Indiana University (moderator)    Panel 2. The Declaration\, Americ
 an Territories\, and Latin America 1:30 to 3:00 p.m.Guy-Uriel Charles\, Ha
 rvard University  Christina D. Ponsa-Kraus\, Columbia University  Emmanuel
  Hiram Arnaud\, Boston University  Luis Fuentes Rohwer\, Indiana Universit
 y (moderator)
GEO:39.167655;-86.523537
LAST-MODIFIED:20260925T184348Z
LOCATION:INDIANA MEMORIAL UNION (UNION BUILDING)
URL:https://events.iu.edu/lawiub/event/2428181-such-now-the-necessity-the-
 declaration-and-secession
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  The symposium considers the Declaration 
 specifically as a secessionist document: how it functioned in the early Un
 ited States\, how it relates to broader theories of secession\, and how it
  has been or might be used in other political contexts.</p><p>  The Declar
 ation of Independence is revered as the formative expression of American v
 alues. However\, it was designed primarily as a legal and rhetorical addre
 ss to the world—"to the opinions of mankind"—justifying a radical proj
 ect: secession. The Declaration advances the two principal arguments that 
 are familiar justifications used by secession movements today: an inherent
  right to self-government based on "consent of the governed\," and "remedi
 al secession" in response to "a long train of abuses." Given this founding
  DNA\, why is the United States no longer a pro-secession advocate? What w
 ould it mean to take the Declaration's arguments seriously today—both in
  America's foreign relations and for America's own territories (such as Pu
 erto Rico) and the 50 states?</p><p>  Schedule:</p><p>  Panel 1. The Decla
 ration and Secession in the early United States 10:30 to 12:00</p><ul><li>
 Kon Dierks\, Indiana University  </li><li>Eric Slauter\, University of Chi
 cago  </li><li>Noah Eber-Schmidt\, Indiana University  </li><li>Timothy Wi
 lliam Waters\, Indiana University (moderator)  </li></ul><p>  Panel 2. The
  Declaration\, American Territories\, and Latin America 1:30 to 3:00 p.m.<
 /p><ul><li>Guy-Uriel Charles\, Harvard University  </li><li>Christina D. P
 onsa-Kraus\, Columbia University  </li><li>Emmanuel Hiram Arnaud\, Boston 
 University  </li><li>Luis Fuentes Rohwer\, Indiana University (moderator) 
  </li></ul>
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<p>\n  Prof. Tim Waters\, <a href="mailto:tiwater
 s@iu.edu">tiwaters@iu.edu</a><br />\n  Assoc. Dean Luis Fuentes-Rohwer\, <
 a href="mailto:lfr@iu.edu">lfr@iu.edu</a>&#160\;\n</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2428181
X-LIVEWHALE-TAGS:America 250
X-LIVEWHALE-TIMEZONE:America/New_York
X-LIVEWHALE-TYPE:events
X-SOURCE:IU Maurer School of Law
END:VEVENT
