BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 261": "https://events.iu.edu/libraries/event/2428178-lecture-lessons-from-a-rapid-response-web-archiving-"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_261
SUMMARY:Lecture: "Lessons from a Rapid-Response Web-Archiving Effort" with
  Anna Kijas
DTSTART:20261002T180000Z
DTEND:20261002T193000Z
DTSTAMP:20260923T145730Z
UID:20261002T180000Z-2428178@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261002T180000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/261/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,501,498/32679_kijas.rev.1790191504.png
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:Anna E. Kijas is Assistant Director of Digital Scholarship and
  Lilly Music Library at Tufts University. She co-founded Saving Ukrainian 
 Cultural Heritage Online\, an initiative focused on safeguarding and prese
 rving the digital cultural heritage of Ukraine during the Russo-Ukrainian 
 War.  Anna's work is centered on principles of social justice\, access\, a
 nd sustainability in relation to digital humanities\, historical (music) r
 esearch\, cultural heritage\, and information studies. Her recent publicat
 ions include a special issue\, "Cultural Heritage in Crisis\," for the jou
 rnal Library Trends\, a co-authored chapter\, "Nimble Tents and Bunkers: T
 he Role of Libraries in Rapid-Response DH\," in The Routledge Companion to
  Libraries\, Archives\, and the Digital Humanities\, a book entitled The L
 ife and Music of Teresa Carreño (1853-1917): A Guide to Research\, and op
 en-access digital projects\, including Documenting Teresa Carreño and Reb
 alancing the Music Canon.  Cosponsored by The Byrnes Institute (REEI)\, IU
  Libraries\, and The Department of Information and Library Science.
GEO:39.170011;-86.517418
LAST-MODIFIED:20260925T150238Z
LOCATION:Hamilton Lugar School of Global and International Studies
URL:https://events.iu.edu/libraries/event/2428178-lecture-lessons-from-a-r
 apid-response-web-archiving-
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Anna E. Kijas is Assistant Director of D
 igital Scholarship and Lilly Music Library at Tufts University. She co-fou
 nded Saving Ukrainian Cultural Heritage Online\, an initiative focused on 
 safeguarding and preserving the digital cultural heritage of Ukraine durin
 g the Russo-Ukrainian War.</p><p>  Anna's work is centered on principles o
 f social justice\, access\, and sustainability in relation to digital huma
 nities\, historical (music) research\, cultural heritage\, and information
  studies. Her recent publications include a special issue\, "Cultural Heri
 tage in Crisis\," for the journal Library Trends\, a co-authored chapter\,
  "Nimble Tents and Bunkers: The Role of Libraries in Rapid-Response DH\," 
 in The Routledge Companion to Libraries\, Archives\, and the Digital Human
 ities\, a book entitled The Life and Music of Teresa Carreño (1853-1917):
  A Guide to Research\, and open-access digital projects\, including Docume
 nting Teresa Carreño and Rebalancing the Music Canon.</p><p>  Cosponsored
  by The Byrnes Institute (REEI)\, IU Libraries\, and The Department of Inf
 ormation and Library Science.</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<p>\n  Laikin Dantchenko\, <a href="mailto:ldantc
 h@iu.edu">ldantch@iu.edu</a>&#160\;\n</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-COST:Free
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2428178
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/261/width/168/heigh
 t/168/crop/1/src_region/0\,0\,501\,498/32679_kijas.rev.1790191504.png
X-LIVEWHALE-SUMMARY:<p>Anna Kijas\, co-founder of Saving Ukrainian Cultura
 l Heritage Online (SUCHO)\, will discuss this rapid-response web-archiving
  effort\, how it drew together a community of activists\, and what the glo
 bal cultural heritage community can learn from the invasion of Ukraine to 
 plan for more resilient futures for digital cultural heritage protection.<
 /p>
X-LIVEWHALE-TAGS:archives|Arts and Humanities|cultural heritage|Digital Hu
 manities|digital scholarship|music history|Music Library|Ukraine
X-LIVEWHALE-TIMEZONE:America/New_York
X-LIVEWHALE-TYPE:events
X-SOURCE:IU Bloomington Libraries
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Lotusfest": "https://www.lotusfest.org/event/arts-village-2026/"}
X-SOURCE-ID:lotusfest
X-SOURCE:Lotus Festival
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261002T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261003T220000
DTSTAMP:20260523T141820Z
CREATED:20260523T141803Z
LAST-MODIFIED:20260523T141820Z
UID:27619-1790964000-1791064800@www.lotusfest.org
SUMMARY:Arts Village
DESCRIPTION:Friday October 2nd from 6PM – 10PM\nSaturday October 3rd from 6PM – 10PM\nKirkwood on the Square (Between College Ave and Walnut)\nThe Arts Village is where creativity comes to life during the festival weekend. Wander through a vibrant evening atmosphere filled with live art demonstrations\, interactive activities\, local and regional artists\, and a Night Bazaar celebrating handmade work\, creativity\, and community. Discover new artists\, try your hand at creative experiences\, and enjoy the energy of the festival after dark. \nDuring the festival-wide break at 8:30 PM Friday evening\, enjoy a special Taste of the IU Drumline bringing rhythm and excitement to the heart of downtown. Then on Saturday evening\, the 2026 Parade returns\, lighting up the streets with music\, movement\, and celebration once again. \nThe Arts Village is alive from 6 PM – 10 PM on Friday\, October 2nd and Saturday\, October 3rd\, 2026. This is a free event to attend. Donations are appreciated and help support the mission of the Lotus Education and Arts Foundation. \nInterested in participating as an artist? Email KP at associatedirector@lotusfest.org.
URL:https://www.lotusfest.org/event/arts-village-2026/
CATEGORIES:Exhibits & Installations,Free Event,Arts Activity
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/webp:https://www.lotusfest.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/art-village.webp
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Campuslabs Iub": "https://beinvolved.indiana.edu/event/12554979"}
X-SOURCE-ID:campuslabs_iub
X-SOURCE:IU beINvolved Student Orgs
CATEGORIES:Cultural
CATEGORIES:Social Event
CATEGORIES:Arts & Humanities
CATEGORIES:Tradition & Community
CATEGORIES:Cultural Wellness
CATEGORIES:Engage & Thrive - Community
CATEGORIES:Campus-Wide Event
DESCRIPTION:The term Mehendi is the Indian word for Henna\, and it is also
  the term used to describe the art of applying henna. Come and learn the 
 basic designs and application techniques of Henna or Mehendi\, which is u
 sed to create temporary tattoos. Please feel free to bring your friends t
 o learn more about Desi culture and the significance of Mehendi in the In
 dian culture! If you come with a group of more than 7 members\, please em
 ail acc@iu.edu in advance so we can prepare the henna materials.   \n \nA
