BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"IU Arts & Humanities Institute": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2369290-easc-colloquium-the-courtesans-memory-voice-and", "IU Hamilton Lugar School": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2369290-easc-colloquium-the-courtesans-memory-voice-and", "IU Kelley School of Business": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2369290-easc-colloquium-the-courtesans-memory-voice-and"}
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 130": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2369290-easc-colloquium-the-courtesans-memory-voice-and"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_130
SUMMARY:EASC Colloquium: The Courtesan's Memory\, Voice\, and Late Ming Dr
 ama - Peng Xu
DTSTART:20261005T160000Z
DTEND:20261005T173000Z
DTSTAMP:20260805T113306Z
UID:20261005T160000Z-2370431@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261005T160000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/140/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,368,367/31730_Book_Cover_Image.rev.1785847682.png
CATEGORIES:Culture and languages,Lectures, talks, seminars, and conference
 s
DESCRIPTION:Peng Xu's The Courtesan's Memory\, Voice\, and Late Ming Drama
  argues that courtesans of the era played an active role in the theater an
 d their impact manifested in Chinese literary history\, albeit concealed o
 r erased. In the late Ming dynasty (1368–1644)\, theater was dominated b
 y male performers with professional actresses largely absent from the stag
 e\, unlike in the Yuan dynasty (1271–1368)\, where women performed witho
 ut restriction in theater troupes. Despite the prevailing assumption that 
 women played no part in the theatrical movement\, The Courtesan's Memory\,
  Voice\, and Late Ming Drama illuminates how courtesans\, as serious theat
 er artists and playwrights\, left a significant mark on the male-dominated
  dramatic and literary landscapes. This study profoundly remaps the textbo
 ok narratives of gender bias\, challenges modern prejudices prevalent in t
 he literary history of Chinese drama\, and reveals how theatrical conditio
 ns and writing styles shifted to accommodate courtesan performances.  Util
 izing a wide range of sources such as scripts\, city guides\, biographies\
 , diaries\, letters\, wood-block illustrations\, and paintings\, this stud
 y distinguishes itself from earlier studies of Chinese courtesans by not t
 hematizing courtesans for their allegorical values\, but rather pinpointin
 g their agency in shaping the features of Chinese theater and recognizing 
 courtesans as a source of creativity and development in the medium. Xu's f
 ocus on courtesan performance culture allows for fresh readings of canonic
 al Chinese plays\, opening up new ways of understanding the history of Chi
 nese drama written by women\, thus deviating from standard historiography 
 based solely on the writings of men.About the author: Peng Xu received her
  Ph.D. from the University of Chicago and was a postdoctoral fellow at UC 
 Berkeley. Currently Associate Professor at ShanghaiTech University\, she h
 as held academic appointments at institutions across the U.S. and Asia. Sh
 e actively promote cross-disciplinary and transnational dialogues in Chine
 se literary and performance studies\, with a particular emphasis on the ag
 ency of actresses and women poets. She has published in leading journals i
 ncluding Harvard Journal of Asiatic Studies\, Late Imperial China\, T'oung
  Pao\, CLEAR: Chinese Literature\, Essays\, and Reviews\, and Ars Oriental
 is. Her research-related activities have been funded by the Kyujanggak Ins
 titute at Seoul National University (2021)\, Fulbright American Scholar pr
 ogram (2020)\, ACLS/Henry-Luce Foundation (2018)\, among others.  An award
 -winning scholar-performer specializing in Kunqu and Peking Opera\, Dr. Xu
  integrates scholarship and stage practice\, bringing historical insight a
 nd aesthetic precision into every performance.
GEO:39.170002;-86.517492
LAST-MODIFIED:20260805T153306Z
LOCATION:GLOBAL &amp\; INTERNATIONAL STUDIES BUILDING
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2369290-easc-colloquium-the-courtesa
 ns-memory-voice-and
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong>Peng Xu</strong>'s <em>The Courtes
 an's Memory\, Voice\, and Late Ming Drama</em> argues that courtesans of t
 he era played an active role in the theater and their impact manifested in
  Chinese literary history\, albeit concealed or erased. In the late Ming d
 ynasty (1368–1644)\, theater was dominated by male performers with profe
 ssional actresses largely absent from the stage\, unlike in the Yuan dynas
 ty (1271–1368)\, where women performed without restriction in theater tr
 oupes. Despite the prevailing assumption that women played no part in the 
 theatrical movement\, <em>The Courtesan's Memory\, Voice\, and Late Ming D
 rama</em> illuminates how courtesans\, as serious theater artists and play
 wrights\, left a significant mark on the male-dominated dramatic and liter
 ary landscapes. This study profoundly remaps the textbook narratives of ge
 nder bias\, challenges modern prejudices prevalent in the literary history
  of Chinese drama\, and reveals how theatrical conditions and writing styl
 es shifted to accommodate courtesan performances.</p><p>  Utilizing a wide
  range of sources such as scripts\, city guides\, biographies\, diaries\, 
 letters\, wood-block illustrations\, and paintings\, this study distinguis
 hes itself from earlier studies of Chinese courtesans by not thematizing c
 ourtesans for their allegorical values\, but rather pinpointing their agen
 cy in shaping the features of Chinese theater and recognizing courtesans a
 s a source of creativity and development in the medium. Xu's focus on cour
 tesan performance culture allows for fresh readings of canonical Chinese p
 lays\, opening up new ways of understanding the history of Chinese drama w
 ritten by women\, thus deviating from standard historiography based solely
  on the writings of men.</p><p><strong>About the author:</strong> Peng Xu 
 received her Ph.D. from the University of Chicago and was a postdoctoral f
 ellow at UC Berkeley. Currently Associate Professor at ShanghaiTech Univer
 sity\, she has held academic appointments at institutions across the U.S. 
 and Asia. She actively promote cross-disciplinary and transnational dialog
 ues in Chinese literary and performance studies\, with a particular emphas
 is on the agency of actresses and women poets. She has published in leadin
 g journals including Harvard Journal of Asiatic Studies\, Late Imperial Ch
 ina\, T'oung Pao\, CLEAR: Chinese Literature\, Essays\, and Reviews\, and 
 Ars Orientalis. Her research-related activities have been funded by the Ky
 ujanggak Institute at Seoul National University (2021)\, Fulbright America
 n Scholar program (2020)\, ACLS/Henry-Luce Foundation (2018)\, among other
 s.</p><p>  An award-winning scholar-performer specializing in Kunqu and Pe
 king Opera\, Dr. Xu integrates scholarship and stage practice\, bringing h
 istorical insight and aesthetic precision into every performance.</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<p>easc@iu.edu</p>
