SUMMARY:Ostrom Workshop | Colloquium: Andrew Flachs
DTSTART:20261005T160000Z
DTEND:20261005T173000Z
DTSTAMP:20260923T152506Z
UID:20261005T160000Z-2428214@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261005T160000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/105/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,640,426/12533_globe-1130870_640.rev.1659031295.jpg
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:Bio  Dr. Andrew Flachs researches food and agriculture systems
 \, exploring genetically modified crops\, heirloom seeds\, and our own mic
 robiomes. An associate professor of anthropology at Purdue University\, hi
 s work among farmers in North America\, the Balkans\, and South India inve
 stigates ecological knowledge and technological change in agricultural sys
 tems. Funded by institutions including the National Geographic Society\, t
 he American Institute of Indian Studies\, the National Science Foundation\
 , and the Social Science Research Council\, Andrew's work has resulted in 
 over three dozen peer-reviewed publications\, five books\, and public-faci
 ng work in venues including Sapiens\, Civil Eats\, Salon\, and the Nationa
 l Geographic magazine. Outside of academia\, he is an avid cook\, cyclist\
 , embarrassing dad to three children\, and musician who has performed in N
 ew York City\, St. Louis\, Asheville\, and the San Francisco Bay Area.Titl
 e  Agriculture as social reproduction: Identifying mutually reinforcing di
 versity in small farmsAbstract  Social reproduction theory illuminates how
  the material and social fabrics of society are formed\, recognizing value
  that is otherwise externalized in capitalist accounting. While it offers 
 a robust vocabulary for describing how class\, identity\, and other ways o
 f being in society are formed\, this approach rarely places lived experien
 ces within larger political ecologies. In this talk\, I consider both how 
 social reproduction reproduces socioeconomic conditions and class relation
 s\, as well as how this work reproduces physical landscapes that generate 
 future possibilities for organizing life. Agriculture is a particularly il
 luminating case to describe how work reproduces social relationships while
  continually creating a landscape in which diverse life can thrive (or suf
 fer). Across India\, Bosnia\, and the US Midwest\, three different sorts o
 f farms with three very different regional histories\, I explore the role 
 of commons and public infrastructure in creating diverse ecologies that su
 stain diverse social relations that sustain diverse economies.About the Co
 lloquium SeriesEach Monday throughout the academic year\, speakers share a
 nd discuss cutting edge research echoing the themes of the Ostrom Workshop
 's research program. Topics range from the governance of space\, cybersecu
 rity\, and data management\, to the environment\, political economy\, and 
 sustainable food science. Presenters are from IU and around the world. Joi
 n us at the workshop or over Zoom to learn how the Ostrom legacy is evolvi
 ng today. For more information\, see ourwebsite (https://ostromworkshop.in
 diana.edu/index.html).
GEO:39.171057;-86.524959
LAST-MODIFIED:20260923T192506Z
LOCATION:The Ostrom Workshop\, 513 N PARK AVE
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2295100-ostrom-workshop-colloquium-a
 ndrew-flachs
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong><span data-contrast="none"><pictur
 e class="lw_image lw_image31396  lw_align_left lw_block"><source type="ima
 ge/webp" srcset="https://events.iu.edu/live/image/scale/2x/gid/105/width/3
 00/height/451/31396_andrew-flachs1.rev.1782501670.webp 2x\, https://events
 .iu.edu/live/image/scale/3x/gid/105/width/300/height/451/31396_andrew-flac
 hs1.rev.1782501670.webp 3x" data-origin="responsive"/><source type="image/
 jpeg" srcset="https://events.iu.edu/live/image/scale/2x/gid/105/width/300/
 height/451/31396_andrew-flachs1.rev.1782501670.jpg 2x\, https://events.iu.
 edu/live/image/scale/3x/gid/105/width/300/height/451/31396_andrew-flachs1.
 rev.1782501670.jpg 3x" data-origin="responsive"/><img width="300" height="
 451" alt="" data-alt="" data-decoration="true" src="https://events.iu.edu/
 live/image/gid/105/width/300/height/451/31396_andrew-flachs1.rev.178250167
 0.jpg" srcset="https://events.iu.edu/live/image/scale/2x/gid/105/width/300
 /height/451/31396_andrew-flachs1.rev.1782501670.jpg 2x\, https://events.iu
 .edu/live/image/scale/3x/gid/105/width/300/height/451/31396_andrew-flachs1
 .rev.1782501670.jpg 3x" data-max-w="2129" data-max-h="3200" loading="lazy"
  data-optimized="true"/></picture></span></strong></p><p><strong><span dat
 a-contrast="none">Bio</span></strong></p><p>  Dr. Andrew Flachs researches
  food and agriculture systems\, exploring genetically modified crops\, hei
 rloom seeds\, and our own microbiomes. An associate professor of anthropol
 ogy at Purdue University\, his work among farmers in North America\, the B
 alkans\, and South India investigates ecological knowledge and technologic
 al change in agricultural systems. Funded by institutions including the Na
 tional Geographic Society\, the American Institute of Indian Studies\, the
  National Science Foundation\, and the Social Science Research Council\, A
 ndrew's work has resulted in over three dozen peer-reviewed publications\,
  five books\, and public-facing work in venues including Sapiens\, Civil E
 ats\, Salon\, and the National Geographic magazine. Outside of academia\, 
 he is an avid cook\, cyclist\, embarrassing dad to three children\, and mu
 sician who has performed in New York City\, St. Louis\, Asheville\, and th
 e San Francisco Bay Area.</p><p><strong>Title</strong></p><p>  Agriculture
  as social reproduction: Identifying mutually reinforcing diversity in sma
 ll farms</p><p><strong>Abstract</strong></p><p>  Social reproduction theor
 y illuminates how the material and social fabrics of society are formed\, 
 recognizing value that is otherwise externalized in capitalist accounting.
  While it offers a robust vocabulary for describing how class\, identity\,
  and other ways of being in society are formed\, this approach rarely plac
 es lived experiences within larger political ecologies. In this talk\, I c
 onsider both how social reproduction reproduces socioeconomic conditions a
 nd class relations\, as well as how this work reproduces physical landscap
 es that generate future possibilities for organizing life. Agriculture is 
 a particularly illuminating case to describe how work reproduces social re
 lationships while continually creating a landscape in which diverse life c
 an thrive (or suffer). Across India\, Bosnia\, and the US Midwest\, three 
 different sorts of farms with three very different regional histories\, I 
 explore the role of commons and public infrastructure in creating diverse 
 ecologies that sustain diverse social relations that sustain diverse econo
 mies.</p><p><strong><span data-contrast="none">About the Colloquium Series
 </span></strong></p><p><span data-contrast="none">Each Monday throughout t
 he academic year\, speakers share and discuss cutting edge research echoin
 g the themes of the Ostrom Workshop's research program. Topics range from 
 the governance of space\, cybersecurity\, and data management\, to the env
 ironment\, political economy\, and sustainable food science. Presenters ar
 e from IU and around the world. Join us at the workshop or over Zoom to le
 arn how the Ostrom legacy is evolving today. For more information\, see ou
 r</span><span data-contrast="none"><a class="external" href="https://ostro
 mworkshop.indiana.edu/index.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">website</
 a></span><span data-contrast="none">.</span></p>
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<p>\n  Karen Mack\, Ostrom Workshop Events Coordi
 nator\, workshop@iu.edu\n</p>
