X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Brown County State Park": "on.in.gov/browncountysp"}
X-SOURCE-ID:localist_brown_county_sp
SUMMARY:Autumn Leaves Revisited\, Brown County State Park
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261006T150000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261006T154500
UID:362012000d4402a2cba38ae40db836ea@events.in.gov
DESCRIPTION:Have you ever wondered how and why our forests change into the
 ir beautiful fall colors? This program explains just that! Join a naturali
 st as we walk through how leaves transform through the seasons and why the
 y turn into the colors we see in the fall on the microscopic level! Stick 
 around after the program for a fun coloring activity to help you recognize
  and remember what changes our green leaves into yellows\, oranges\, reds\
 , and browns.
LOCATION:Brown County State Park\, Nature Center program room\, 1405 S.R. 
 46 W\, Nashville\, IN 
URL:on.in.gov/browncountysp
X-SOURCE:Brown County State Park
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"IU Arts & Humanities Institute": "https://events.iu.edu/cahi/event/2421208-lunch-and-moderated-qampa-with-carol-anderson", "IU Neal-Marshall Black Culture Center": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2421208-lunch-and-moderated-qampa-with-carol-anderson"}
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 130": "https://events.iu.edu/cahi/event/2421208-lunch-and-moderated-qampa-with-carol-anderson"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_130
SUMMARY:Lunch and Moderated Q&A with Carol Anderson
DTSTART:20261006T153000Z
DTEND:20261006T170000Z
DTSTAMP:20260917T104425Z
UID:20261006T153000Z-2421208@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261006T153000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/130/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,1200,1500/32566_Anderson_social_media_headshot.rev.178965
 4744.jpg
CATEGORIES:Career development,Functions, festivals, and celebrations,Lectu
 res, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:Carol Anderson will join Randal Jelks (https://aaads.indiana.e
 du/about/faculty/jelks-randal.html) (Ruth N. Halls Professor of African Am
 erican and African Diaspora Studies) for a conversation about her research
  and career as a public writer\, reflecting on the importance of intersect
 ing the arts and humanities with civic engagement. The discussion will be 
 followed by an audience Q&amp\;A\, and this event will be limited to the f
 irst 60 people to register.  Professor Anderson will also give a public le
 cture\, "One Person\, No Vote: How Voter Suppression is Destroying Our Dem
 ocracy\," at the Buskirk-Chumley Theater on Tuesday\, October 6 at 7pm. Th
 e event is free\, but ticketed. Tickets are available at the BCT's website
  (https://buskirkchumley.org/events/one-person-no-vote-a-lecture-and-q-a-w
 ith-carol-anderson-october-6-2026/) and box office.  Carol Anderson is the
  Robert W. Woodruff Professor of African American Studies at Emory Univers
 ity. She is the author of several bestselling books including: The Second:
  Race and Guns in a Fatally Unequal America (2020)\; One Person\, No Vote:
  How Voter Suppression is Destroying Our Democracy (2018)\, which was long
 -listed for the National Book Award in Non-Fiction\; and the critically-ac
 claimed White Rage: The Unspoken Truth of Our Racial Divide (2016)\, which
  won the 2016 National Book Critics Circle Award for Criticism\, was a New
  York Times Editor's Pick\, and was listed on the Zora List of 100 Best Bo
 oks by Black Women Authors since 1850.  Professor Anderson is a member of 
 the American Academy of Arts and Sciences and the American Philosophical S
 ociety\, and a recipient of the Ella Baker Lifetime Achievement Award from
  the Hurston/Wright Foundation. Her groundbreaking research has been suppo
 rted by fellowships from the American Council of Learned Societies\, the F
 ord Foundation\, National Humanities Center\, Harvard University\, and the
  Gilder Lehrman Institute of American History.  This event is presented wi
 th generous support from the College of Arts &amp\; Sciences and the Ruth 
 N. Halls Humanities Fund.
LAST-MODIFIED:20260922T163717Z
LOCATION:Maxwell Hall
URL:https://events.iu.edu/cahi/event/2421208-lunch-and-moderated-qampa-wit
 h-carol-anderson
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Carol Anderson will join <a href="https:
 //aaads.indiana.edu/about/faculty/jelks-randal.html">Randal Jelks</a> (Rut
 h N. Halls Professor of African American and African Diaspora Studies) for
  a conversation about her research and career as a public writer\, reflect
 ing on the importance of intersecting the arts and humanities with civic e
 ngagement. The discussion will be followed by an audience Q&amp\;A\, and t
 his event will be limited to the first 60 people to register.</p><p>  Prof
 essor Anderson will also give a public lecture\, "One Person\, No Vote: Ho
 w Voter Suppression is Destroying Our Democracy\," at the Buskirk-Chumley 
 Theater on Tuesday\, October 6 at 7pm. The event is free\, but ticketed. T
 ickets are available at the BCT's <a href="https://buskirkchumley.org/even
 ts/one-person-no-vote-a-lecture-and-q-a-with-carol-anderson-october-6-2026
 /">website</a> and box office.</p><p>  Carol Anderson is the Robert W. Woo
 druff Professor of African American Studies at Emory University. She is th
 e author of several bestselling books including: <em>The Second: Race and 
 Guns in a Fatally Unequal America</em> (2020)\; <em>One Person\, No Vote: 
 How Voter Suppression is Destroying Our Democracy</em> (2018)\, which was 
 long-listed for the National Book Award in Non-Fiction\; and the criticall
 y-acclaimed <em>White Rage: The Unspoken Truth of Our Racial Divide</em> (
 2016)\, which won the 2016 National Book Critics Circle Award for Criticis
 m\, was a <em>New York Times</em> Editor's Pick\, and was listed on the Zo
 ra List of 100 Best Books by Black Women Authors since 1850.</p><p>  Profe
 ssor Anderson is a member of the American Academy of Arts and Sciences and
  the American Philosophical Society\, and a recipient of the Ella Baker Li
 fetime Achievement Award from the Hurston/Wright Foundation. Her groundbre
 aking research has been supported by fellowships from the American Council
  of Learned Societies\, the Ford Foundation\, National Humanities Center\,
  Harvard University\, and the Gilder Lehrman Institute of American History
 .</p><p>  This event is presented with generous support from the College o
 f Arts &amp\; Sciences and the Ruth N. Halls Humanities Fund.</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<p>\n  College Arts + Humanities Institute<br />\
 n  Gayle Karch Cook Center for Public Arts and Humanities<br />\n  Maxwell
  Hall\, 750 E. Kirkwood Avenue<br />\n  Indiana University\, Bloomington<b
 r />\n  <br />\n  web: <a href="https://cahi.indiana.edu">cahi.indiana.edu
 </a><br />\n  email: <a href="mailto:cahi@iu.edu">cahi@iu.edu</a>\n</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-COST:Free
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2421208
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/130/width/168/heigh
 t/168/crop/1/src_region/0\,0\,1200\,1500/32566_Anderson_social_media_heads
 hot.rev.1789654744.jpg
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE-CAPTION:Portrait of Carol Anderson
X-LIVEWHALE-RSVP:1
X-LIVEWHALE-SUMMARY:<p>The College Arts + Humanities institute is delighte
 d to host a lunch and moderated Q&amp\;A with <a href="https://aas.emory.e
 du/people/bios/faculty/anderson-carol.html">Carol Anderson</a>\, bestselli
 ng author\, preeminent historian of African American history and oppressio
 n\, and an influential voice of civil and voting rights.</p>
