X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 135": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2425020-mangrove-school-makongocaterpillars-wqa-for-all-the-"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_135
SUMMARY:Mangrove School + Makongo/Caterpillars w/Q&A | For All the Childre
 n
DTSTART:20261006T230000Z
DTEND:20261007T000000Z
DTSTAMP:20260923T152451Z
UID:20261006T230000Z-2428205@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261006T230000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/81/width/168/height/168/crop/1
 /src_region/0,0,1920,1080/32617_Mangrove_School__Caterpillars_1920x1080.re
 v.1790005231.jpg
CATEGORIES:Culture and languages,Performances, shows, and films
DESCRIPTION:Mangrove School(Filipa César and Sónia Vaz Borges\, Guinea B
 issau\, 2022): During the anticolonial struggle against Portuguese occupat
 ion\, the free schools in Guinea Bissau moved deep into the jungle and fou
 nd the mangroves. The schools were fashioned on the interwoven branches an
 d roots. The directors film the mangroves as an infrastructure with a netw
 orked architecture\, as the locus of transmission\, of radical education\,
  attentive to the memory of the struggles. The mangrove's branches are pow
 erful and encourage networks\, collectives\, and horizontality. [35 min\; 
 documentary\; Portuguese with English subtitles]Makongo/Caterpillars(Elvis
  Sabin Ngaïbino\, Central African Republic\, 2020): Albert and André str
 ive to create a school in their forest community in equatorial Central Afr
 ican Republic. As the only secondary school graduates in their village\, t
 hey teach local children while relying on the annual makongo (caterpillar)
  harvest to fund their project. This vivid ethnographic portrait offers no
  embellishment\, no narration\, and no added music. The direct cinema appr
 oach is remarkably sensitive\, attuned to the sounds of animals\, the rain
  forest\, and the daily life of the men and their families. [73 min\; docu
 mentary\; Sango and French with English subtitles]A Q&amp\;A will follow t
 he screening.
GEO:39.168613;-86.517376
LAST-MODIFIED:20260923T192451Z
LOCATION:IU CINEMA
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2425020-mangrove-school-makongocater
 pillars-wqa-for-all-the-
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><i><strong>Mangrove School</strong></i><st
 rong>(Filipa César and Sónia Vaz Borges\, Guinea Bissau\, 2022):</strong
 > During the anticolonial struggle against Portuguese occupation\, the fre
 e schools in Guinea Bissau moved deep into the jungle and found the mangro
 ves. The schools were fashioned on the interwoven branches and roots. The 
 directors film the mangroves as an infrastructure with a networked archite
 cture\, as the locus of transmission\, of radical education\, attentive to
  the memory of the struggles. The mangrove's branches are powerful and enc
 ourage networks\, collectives\, and horizontality. [35 min\; documentary\;
  Portuguese with English subtitles]</p><p><i><strong>Makongo</strong></i><
 strong>/</strong><i><strong>Caterpillars</strong></i><strong>(Elvis Sabin 
 Ngaïbino\, Central African Republic\, 2020):</strong> Albert and André s
 trive to create a school in their forest community in equatorial Central A
 frican Republic. As the only secondary school graduates in their village\,
  they teach local children while relying on the annual makongo (caterpilla
 r) harvest to fund their project. This vivid ethnographic portrait offers 
 no embellishment\, no narration\, and no added music. The direct cinema ap
 proach is remarkably sensitive\, attuned to the sounds of animals\, the ra
 in forest\, and the daily life of the men and their families. [73 min\; do
 cumentary\; Sango and French with English subtitles]</p><p><i><strong>A Q&
 amp\;A will follow the screening.</strong></i></p>
