X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 135": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2382679-ostrom-workshop-research-series-kyuwoong-kyeong"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_135
SUMMARY:Ostrom Workshop | Research Series: Kyuwoong Kyeong
DTSTART:20261007T170000Z
DTEND:20261007T180000Z
DTSTAMP:20260925T151201Z
UID:20261007T170000Z-2431145@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261007T170000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/105/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,298,276/12535_research_book_spin_image.rev.1659037749.png
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:Bio  Kyuwoong Kyeong is a Ph.D. candidate at the O'Neill Schoo
 l of Public and Environmental Affairs at Indiana University Bloomington. H
 is research explores how public employees and citizens respond to complex 
 administrative contexts in which tensions arise among rules\, discretion\,
  and public values. His research interests include behavioral public admin
 istration\, public sector decision-making\, prosocial behavior\, and citiz
 en evaluations of government. Focusing on prosocial rule-breaking and whis
 tleblowing\, his dissertation examines how the meaning and consequences of
  these behaviors for citizens and public organizations vary across adminis
 trative and institutional contexts.Title  Expecting Cold Decisions\, but D
 esiring Warm Bureaucrats? Evidence from Two Survey Experiments on U.S. Cit
 izensAbstract  Prosocial rule-breaking (PSRB) by public employees—the in
 tentional violation of formal rules to help others—embodies a fundamenta
 l tension between rule adherence and responsiveness to citizens' welfare. 
 Although prior research has largely examined why bureaucrats engage in PSR
 B\, less is known about how citizens respond when public employees break r
 ules for prosocial purposes. This research draws on two preregistered surv
 ey experiments with U.S. adults to examine how citizens evaluate both rule
 -breaking decisions and the bureaucrats who make them\, and how these eval
 uations vary across contexts. The first experiment focuses on the client\,
  testing whether need and prior effort shape reactions to a bureaucrat's r
 ule-breaking decision. The second focuses on the bureaucrat\, examining wh
 ether competence and consistency in prior decisions alter those reactions.
  Across the two studies\, citizens' evaluations are highly context-depende
 nt. In the first experiment\, PSRB reduces approval of the decision and pe
 rceptions of the bureaucrat's integrity and competence while simultaneousl
 y increasing perceived benevolence and willingness to be served by the bur
 eaucrat. Client need also makes several evaluations of the rule-breaking b
 ureaucrat more favorable. In the second experiment\, however\, PSRB increa
 ses approval of the decision\, overall trustworthiness\, and willingness t
 o be served\, while continuing to increase perceived benevolence and reduc
 e perceived integrity. These favorable responses are stronger when the bur
 eaucrat is highly competent. Together\, the findings show that citizens' r
 esponses to bureaucratic rule-breaking depend on characteristics of both t
 he beneficiary and the bureaucrat\, as well as on whether citizens are eva
 luating the decision itself or the bureaucrat who made it.About the Ostrom
  Workshop Research Series  Learn about innovative research by Graduate Stu
 dents and Visiting Scholars as they advance the mission of the Ostrom Work
 shop\, building upon the theme of governance to understand and address maj
 or societal problems.
GEO:39.171057;-86.524959
LAST-MODIFIED:20260925T191201Z
LOCATION:The Ostrom Workshop\, 513 N PARK AVE
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2382679-ostrom-workshop-research-ser
 ies-kyuwoong-kyeong
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><picture class="lw_image lw_image31982  lw
 _align_left lw_block"><source type="image/webp" srcset="https://events.iu.
 edu/live/image/scale/2x/gid/105/width/300/height/300/crop/1/src_region/0\,
 25\,2133\,2156/31982_Photo_Kyeong.rev.1786725055.webp 2x\, https://events.
 iu.edu/live/image/scale/3x/gid/105/width/300/height/300/crop/1/src_region/
 0\,25\,2133\,2156/31982_Photo_Kyeong.rev.1786725055.webp 3x" data-origin="
 responsive"/><source type="image/jpeg" srcset="https://events.iu.edu/live/
 image/scale/2x/gid/105/width/300/height/300/crop/1/src_region/0\,25\,2133\
 ,2156/31982_Photo_Kyeong.rev.1786725055.jpg 2x\, https://events.iu.edu/liv
 e/image/scale/3x/gid/105/width/300/height/300/crop/1/src_region/0\,25\,213
 3\,2156/31982_Photo_Kyeong.rev.1786725055.jpg 3x" data-origin="responsive"
 /><img width="300" height="300" alt="" data-alt="" data-decoration="true" 
 src="https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/105/width/300/height/300/crop/1/
 src_region/0\,25\,2133\,2156/31982_Photo_Kyeong.rev.1786725055.jpg" title=
 "Photo Kyeong" srcset="https://events.iu.edu/live/image/scale/2x/gid/105/w
 idth/300/height/300/crop/1/src_region/0\,25\,2133\,2156/31982_Photo_Kyeong
 .rev.1786725055.jpg 2x\, https://events.iu.edu/live/image/scale/3x/gid/105
 /width/300/height/300/crop/1/src_region/0\,25\,2133\,2156/31982_Photo_Kyeo
 ng.rev.1786725055.jpg 3x" data-max-w="2133" data-max-h="2131" loading="laz
 y" data-optimized="true"/></picture></p><p><strong>Bio</strong></p><p>  Ky
 uwoong Kyeong is a Ph.D. candidate at the O'Neill School of Public and Env
 ironmental Affairs at Indiana University Bloomington. His research explore
 s how public employees and citizens respond to complex administrative cont
 exts in which tensions arise among rules\, discretion\, and public values.
  His research interests include behavioral public administration\, public 
 sector decision-making\, prosocial behavior\, and citizen evaluations of g
 overnment. Focusing on prosocial rule-breaking and whistleblowing\, his di
 ssertation examines how the meaning and consequences of these behaviors fo
 r citizens and public organizations vary across administrative and institu
 tional contexts.</p><p><strong>Title</strong></p><p>  Expecting Cold Decis
 ions\, but Desiring Warm Bureaucrats? Evidence from Two Survey Experiments
  on U.S. Citizens</p><p><strong>Abstract</strong></p><p>  Prosocial rule-b
 reaking (PSRB) by public employees—the intentional violation of formal r
 ules to help others—embodies a fundamental tension between rule adherenc
 e and responsiveness to citizens' welfare. Although prior research has lar
 gely examined why bureaucrats engage in PSRB\, less is known about how cit
 izens respond when public employees break rules for prosocial purposes. Th
 is research draws on two preregistered survey experiments with U.S. adults
  to examine how citizens evaluate both rule-breaking decisions and the bur
 eaucrats who make them\, and how these evaluations vary across contexts. T
 he first experiment focuses on the client\, testing whether need and prior
  effort shape reactions to a bureaucrat's rule-breaking decision. The seco
 nd focuses on the bureaucrat\, examining whether competence and consistenc
 y in prior decisions alter those reactions. Across the two studies\, citiz
 ens' evaluations are highly context-dependent. In the first experiment\, P
 SRB reduces approval of the decision and perceptions of the bureaucrat's i
 ntegrity and competence while simultaneously increasing perceived benevole
 nce and willingness to be served by the bureaucrat. Client need also makes
  several evaluations of the rule-breaking bureaucrat more favorable. In th
 e second experiment\, however\, PSRB increases approval of the decision\, 
 overall trustworthiness\, and willingness to be served\, while continuing 
 to increase perceived benevolence and reduce perceived integrity. These fa
 vorable responses are stronger when the bureaucrat is highly competent. To
 gether\, the findings show that citizens' responses to bureaucratic rule-b
 reaking depend on characteristics of both the beneficiary and the bureaucr
 at\, as well as on whether citizens are evaluating the decision itself or 
 the bureaucrat who made it.</p><p><strong>About the Ostrom Workshop Resear
 ch Series</strong></p><p>  Learn about innovative research by Graduate Stu
 dents and Visiting Scholars as they advance the mission of the Ostrom Work
 shop\, building upon the theme of governance to understand and address maj
 or societal problems.</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<p>\n  Karen Mack\, Ostrom Workshop Marketing &am
 p\; Special Events Coordinator\, <a href="mailto:workshop@indiana.edu">wor
 kshop@indiana.edu</a>&#160\;\n</p>
