 s
DESCRIPTION:Join the Borns Jewish Studies Program for the opening symposiu
 m of Memory Transformed: Materials of Heritage in Jewish Eastern European 
 Life\, a day of scholarship\, art\, and music exploring how Eastern Europe
 an Jewish culture is remembered and continues to shape contemporary artist
 ic production.11:00 a.m. — Complimentary Brunch at the Tudor Room  Atten
 dees are welcome to join us for a brunch before the symposium begins. Brun
 ch is open to all Memory Transformed attendees.12:30–3:00 p.m.  Symposiu
 mDogwood Room\, Indiana Memorial Union  The symposium will feature two pan
 els examining Jewish material culture\, music\, memory\, and the cultural 
 afterlives of objects associated with Eastern European Jewish life. Schola
 rs and artists will explore how recordings\, photographs\, books\, memoria
 ls\, and other cultural objects preserve knowledge\, shape memory\, and in
 spire contemporary cultural production.Panel 1Judith BerksonPatterns of Im
 provisation in Live Cantorial Recordings\, 1950–1980Jeremiah LockwoodPla
 stinkes and idisher rekords: Towards a History of Jewish Sacred Pop Music 
 as Material CultureDov-Ber KerlerFrom the New to the Old World and Vice Ve
 rsa: Contrafacts\, Adaptation and Radical ReworkingPanel 2Halina GoldbergM
 aterial Culture\, Memory\, and the Making of a Museum and Digital Project 
 on Jewish ŁódźNicolette van den BogerdComposing Jewish Musical Material
  Culture After the HolocaustJennifer McComasTransformations of Material an
 d Memory in Ruth Weinberg's Shtetl PortfolioModerators  Joanna Niżyńska 
  Sergio Ospina-Romero  The symposium is part of the larger Memory Transfor
 med program taking place throughout the day on Sunday\, October 11\, inclu
 ding brunch\, an afternoon reception and optional guided museum tours at t
 he Eskenazi Museum of Art\, and an evening concert at Auer Hall.Free and o
 pen to the public. Registration is encouraged.
GEO:39.167655;-86.523537
LAST-MODIFIED:20260922T141056Z
LOCATION:INDIANA MEMORIAL UNION - IU BLOOMINGTON
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2412280-memory-transformed-symposium
 -materials-of-heritage-i
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Join the Borns Jewish Studies Program fo
 r the opening symposium of Memory Transformed: Materials of Heritage in Je
 wish Eastern European Life\, a day of scholarship\, art\, and music explor
 ing how Eastern European Jewish culture is remembered and continues to sha
 pe contemporary artistic production.</p><p><strong>11:00 a.m. — Complime
 ntary Brunch at the Tudor Room</strong><br />  Attendees are welcome to jo
 in us for a brunch before the symposium begins. Brunch is open to all Memo
 ry Transformed attendees.</p><p><strong>12:30–3:00 p.m. — Symposium</s
 trong><br /><strong>Dogwood Room\, Indiana Memorial Union</strong></p><p> 
  The symposium will feature two panels examining Jewish material culture\,
  music\, memory\, and the cultural afterlives of objects associated with E
 astern European Jewish life. Scholars and artists will explore how recordi
 ngs\, photographs\, books\, memorials\, and other cultural objects preserv
 e knowledge\, shape memory\, and inspire contemporary cultural production.
 </p><p><strong>Panel 1</strong></p><p><strong>Judith Berkson</strong><br /
 ><em>Patterns of Improvisation in Live Cantorial Recordings\, 1950–1980<
 /em></p><p><strong>Jeremiah Lockwood</strong><br /><em>Plastinkes and idis
 her rekords: Towards a History of Jewish Sacred Pop Music as Material Cult
 ure</em></p><p><strong>Dov-Ber Kerler</strong><br /><em>From the New to th
 e Old World and Vice Versa: Contrafacts\, Adaptation and Radical Reworking
 </em></p><p><strong>Panel 2</strong></p><p><strong>Halina Goldberg</strong
 ><br /><em>Material Culture\, Memory\, and the Making of a Museum and Digi
 tal Project on Jewish Łódź</em></p><p><strong>Nicolette van den Bogerd<
 /strong><br /><em>Composing Jewish Musical Material Culture After the Holo
 caust</em></p><p><strong>Jennifer McComas</strong><br /><em>Transformation
 s of Material and Memory in Ruth Weinberg's Shtetl Portfolio</em></p><p><s
 trong>Moderators</strong><br />  Joanna Niżyńska<br />  Sergio Ospina-Ro
 mero</p><p>  The symposium is part of the larger Memory Transformed progra
 m taking place throughout the day on Sunday\, October 11\, including brunc
 h\, an afternoon reception and optional guided museum tours at the Eskenaz
 i Museum of Art\, and an evening concert at Auer Hall.</p><p><strong>Free 
 and open to the public. Registration is encouraged.</strong></p>
