BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"IU Arts & Humanities Institute": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2414877-center-for-refugee-studies-op-ed-initiative", "IU Hamilton Lugar School": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2414877-center-for-refugee-studies-op-ed-initiative"}
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 130": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2414877-center-for-refugee-studies-op-ed-initiative"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_130
SUMMARY:Center for Refugee Studies Op-Ed Initiative Webinar Series
DTSTART:20261019T160000Z
DTEND:20261019T170000Z
DTSTAMP:20260914T120756Z
UID:20261012T160000Z-2416926@events.iu.edu__20261019
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/491/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,2550,3300/32472_flyer_oped.rev.1789140546.png
DESCRIPTION:The CRS Op-Ed Initiative\, open to faculty\, students\, and th
 e CRS community\, teaches participants how to convert research insights in
 to accessible\, public-facing op-eds. This four-course series covers how t
 o develop\, write\, edit\, and publish op-eds\, and why opinion writing is
  an important vessel for spreading innovative ideas and sparking important
  discussions. Participants do not need any prior publication experience to
  join\, and the course is free.The series is led by Assistant Director Mer
 edith Perkins\, who has six years of experience writing over 50 op-eds as 
 a freelance journalist in student\, local\, and national publications. She
  won the 2024 and 2025 Region 4 Society of Professional Journalists Best C
 ollege Opinion Writing award and the 2023 Ohio News Media Association Best
  Collegiate Column Writing award. A current Master of Public Affairs-Maste
 r of International Affairs student\, her past newsroom experience inspires
  her to make complex academic conversations accessible for public audience
 s.Session 1: Before You Writewill introduce the history and purpose of op-
 eds and the range of places where opinion writing appears\, including loca
 l and national newspapers\, industry publications\, departmental blogs\, a
 nd platforms such as Substack. We will review genre conventions of op-eds\
 , why op-eds are useful\, and what information writers should know (e.g. w
 orkplace media policies) before considering publication.Session 2: Draftin
 g Your First Op-Edwill focus on identifying a strong and timely argument. 
 Participants will examine who is already writing about their topic\, the d
 ominant conversation surrounding it\, and recent news or research that mig
 ht create an opportunity for commentary. A central exercise will help part
 icipants identify the "knowledge gap" between what they know through exper
 tise\, research\, or lived experience and what a general reader is likely 
 to know. Participants will use that gap to develop a clear\, relevant argu
 ment for a public audience.Session 3: Editing Your Op-Edwill help particip
 ants turn a draft into an accessible\, publication-ready piece. Topics wil
 l include common word-count requirements\, clear and concise language\, re
 ducing jargon\, checking hyperlinks and sources\, anticipating possible mi
 sunderstandings\, proper image attribution\, headline writing\, and basic 
 search engine optimization. After the session\, participants can send draf
 ts to the instructor for personalized feedback.Session 4: Publishing and P
 itching Your Op-Edwill help participants identify the right outlet for the
 ir work based on the audience they hope to reach. The session will compare
  features and challenges of publishing in local and national newspapers\, 
 industry publications\, institutional blogs\, and independent platforms. P
 articipants will learn what to include in a submission or pitch\, such as 
 a brief description\, author bio\, word count\, contact information\, and 
 headshot\, as well as how to recognize potential red flags. The session wi
 ll conclude with an editor's perspective on what happens behind the inbox\
 , including high submission volume\, response times\, competition for limi
 ted space\, and why many submissions may be read without receiving a reply
 .By the end of the series\, participants should understand how to identify
  a compelling op-ed idea\, communicate specialized knowledge to a general 
 audience\, edit a piece to common publication standards\, and develop a re
 alistic strategy for publication. Participants who work on a piece through
 out the program may leave with a completed or near-completed op-ed and a l
 ist of appropriate outlets to approach.    &#160\;  Register to attend at 
 https://go.iu.edu/cVZV1S (https://go.iu.edu/cVZV1S) to get the Zoom link.
LAST-MODIFIED:20260914T160756Z
LOCATION:Zoom
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2414877-center-for-refugee-studies-o
 p-ed-initiative
