BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"IU Bloomington Libraries": "https://events.iu.edu/libraries/event/2426609-the-cost-of-openness-ai-extraction-and-scholarly", "IU Kelley School of Business": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2426609-the-cost-of-openness-ai-extraction-and-scholarly"}
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 261": "https://events.iu.edu/libraries/event/2426609-the-cost-of-openness-ai-extraction-and-scholarly"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_261
SUMMARY:The Cost of Openness: AI\, Extraction\, and Scholarly Governance
DTSTART:20261020T150000Z
DTEND:20261020T163000Z
DTSTAMP:20260922T110333Z
UID:20261020T150000Z-2426609@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261020T150000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/261/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,3200,3200/32496_oa_week_2026_livewhale.rev.1789410307.jpg
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:Scholarly works are increasingly incorporated into AI systems\
 , often without the consent of the academic communities that created them.
  Questions of access are therefore becoming inseparable from those of gove
 rnance: not simply who can access scholarship\, but who determines how it 
 may be used.  One response has been to strengthen copyright protections\, 
 limit access\, and constrain downstream reuse. But must scholarly communit
 ies choose between predatory extraction and defensive enclosure? Or can sc
 holarship remain broadly accessible and computationally usable while resea
 rch communities exercise agency over how it is used? To explore these ques
 tions\, this panel will bring together perspectives from scholarly communi
 cation\, information ethics\, and author advocacy to consider what an ethi
 cal scholarly communication ecosystem might look like in an era of generat
 ive AI.PanelistsCasey Fiesler (https://caseyfiesler.com/)\, Associate Prof
 essor and William R. Payden Endowed Professor of Information Science at th
 e University of Colorado Boulder  Dave Hansen (https://dhansen.org/)\, Exe
 cutive Director of Authors Alliance  Dr. Samuel A. Moore (https://www.samu
 elmoore.org/)\, Scholarly Communication Specialist at Cambridge University
  Library  ModeratorAngie Raymond (https://kelley.iu.edu/faculty-research/f
 aculty-directory/profile.html?id=angraymo)\, Chair of the Department of Bu
 siness Law and Ethics\, Graf Family Professor\, and Professor of Business 
 Law and Ethics at Indiana University Bloomington\; Director of the Program
  on Data Management and Information Governance at the Ostrom Workshop
LAST-MODIFIED:20260922T173115Z
URL:https://events.iu.edu/libraries/event/2426609-the-cost-of-openness-ai-
 extraction-and-scholarly
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Scholarly works are increasingly incorpo
 rated into AI systems\, often without the consent of the academic communit
 ies that created them. Questions of access are therefore becoming insepara
 ble from those of governance: not simply who can access scholarship\, but 
 who determines how it may be used.</p><p>  One response has been to streng
 then copyright protections\, limit access\, and constrain downstream reuse
 . But must scholarly communities choose between predatory extraction and d
 efensive enclosure? Or can scholarship remain broadly accessible and compu
 tationally usable while research communities exercise agency over how it i
 s used? To explore these questions\, this panel will bring together perspe
 ctives from scholarly communication\, information ethics\, and author advo
 cacy to consider what an ethical scholarly communication ecosystem might l
 ook like in an era of generative AI.</p><p><strong>Panelists</strong></p><
 ul><li><a href="https://caseyfiesler.com/">Casey Fiesler</a>\, Associate P
 rofessor and William R. Payden Endowed Professor of Information Science at
  the University of Colorado Boulder  </li><li><a href="https://dhansen.org
 /">Dave Hansen</a>\, Executive Director of Authors Alliance  </li><li><a h
 ref="https://www.samuelmoore.org/">Dr. Samuel A. Moore</a>\, Scholarly Com
 munication Specialist at Cambridge University Library  </li></ul><p><stron
 g>Moderator</strong></p><ul><li><a href="https://kelley.iu.edu/faculty-res
 earch/faculty-directory/profile.html?id=angraymo">Angie Raymond</a>\, Chai
 r of the Department of Business Law and Ethics\, Graf Family Professor\, a
 nd Professor of Business Law and Ethics at Indiana University Bloomington\
 ; Director of the Program on Data Management and Information Governance at
  the Ostrom Workshop  </li></ul>
X-LIVEWHALE-COST:Free
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2426609
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/261/width/168/heigh
 t/168/crop/1/src_region/0\,0\,3200\,3200/32496_oa_week_2026_livewhale.rev.
 1789410307.jpg
X-LIVEWHALE-IS-ONLINE:1
X-LIVEWHALE-ONLINE-INSTRUCTIONS:<p>\n  You will receive the Zoom link upon
  registration.&#160\;\n</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-ONLINE-TYPE:Online only
X-LIVEWHALE-RSVP:1
X-LIVEWHALE-SUMMARY:<p>\n  Generative AI has intensified a long-standing g
 overnance gap in scholarly communication.\n</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-TAGS:libraries|OAWeek|Open Access Week|open access|Scholarly C
 ommunication
X-LIVEWHALE-TIMEZONE:America/New_York
X-LIVEWHALE-TYPE:events
X-SOURCE:IU Bloomington Libraries, IU Kelley School of Business
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Brown County State Park": "on.in.gov/browncountysp"}
X-SOURCE-ID:localist_brown_county_sp
SUMMARY:Autumn Leaves Revisited\, Brown County State Park
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261020T150000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261020T154500
UID:4932de3e879c634ff0a96b573c708c63@events.in.gov
DESCRIPTION:Have you ever wondered how and why our forests change into the
 ir beautiful fall colors? This program explains just that! Join a naturali
 st as we walk through how leaves transform through the seasons and why the
 y turn into the colors we see in the fall on the microscopic level! Stick 
 around after the program for a fun coloring activity to help you recognize
  and remember what changes our green leaves into yellows\, oranges\, reds\
 , and browns.
LOCATION:Brown County State Park\, Nature Center program room\, 1405 S.R. 
 46 W\, Nashville\, IN 
URL:on.in.gov/browncountysp
X-SOURCE:Brown County State Park
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Brown County State Park": "https://on.in.gov/browncountysp"}
X-SOURCE-ID:localist_brown_county_sp
SUMMARY:Fall Color Hike: Discovery Trail\, Brown County State Park
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261020T150000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261020T154500
UID:1142a507d9d65c4927e3141cd04b48f3@events.in.gov
DESCRIPTION:Join a park naturalist for a guided hike of the Discovery Trai
 l. This is a general hike about a variety of Brown County State Park topic
 s. Learn about Brown County&#39\;s fall colors\, flora\, fauna\, pioneer h
 istory\, and the interesting geology of Brown County State Park. The Disco
 very Trail is about half a mile long and considered moderate. There are mu
 ddy spots\, uneven surfaces\, tree roots\, and mild slopes. The trail will
  take us about 40 minutes to complete.
LOCATION:Brown County State Park\, Nature Center\, 1405 S.R. 46 W\, Nashvi
 lle\, IN 
URL:https://on.in.gov/browncountysp
X-SOURCE:Brown County State Park
END:VEVENT
