X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 135": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2429414-date-change-21jpsi-special-event-the-us-japan-allian"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_135
SUMMARY:**DATE CHANGE** 21JPSI Special Event: "The U.S.-Japan Alliance and
  the Free and Open Indo-Pacific" (Minister ODAIRA (Japan's Ministry of For
 eign Affairs))
DTSTART:20261020T200000Z
DTEND:20261020T211000Z
DTSTAMP:20260925T104645Z
UID:20261020T200000Z-2430768@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261020T200000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/162/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,710,915/32691_20261020_21JPSI_MASATSUGU_ODAIRA_FLYER.rev.
 1790267800.png
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:Minister Odaira's prepared remarks will be followed by a Q&amp
 \;A/discussion moderated by 21JPSI Director Adam P. Liff. **Please note th
 at this event will be held under the Chatham House rule (no recording\, at
 tribution\, or quoting\, please). (https://www.chathamhouse.org/about-us/c
 hatham-house-rule) .**&#160\;  In his first public talk on the subject\, M
 inister for Public Affairs Masatsugu Odaira (Embassy of Japan in Washingto
 n\, DC) will provide his personal assessment of the current and future of 
 U.S.-Japan relations and discuss Japan's Free and Open Indo-Pacific (FOIP)
  vision–which Prime Minister Takaichi updated in a major speech in Vietn
 am this past spring. Against the backdrop of "an increasingly severe inter
 national environment" and intensifying geopolitical competition\, Japan's 
 updated FOIP framework expands Tokyo's vision of maritime security\, digit
 al connectivity\, and economic resilience. It is designed to foster multi-
 ocean collaboration between East Asia\, ASEAN\, South Asia\, the Pacific\,
  and coastal Africa to ensure regional stability.&#160\;This event is open
  to all students\, faculty\, staff\, and members of the community.&#160\;A
 bout the speaker:Mr. Masatsugu ODAIRA\, Minister of Public Affairs at the 
 Embassy of Japan in the United States of America\, assumed his present pos
 ition in September 2025.  Minister Odaira joined the Ministry of Foreign A
 ffairs in 1998 after graduating from Tokyo University's Faculty of Law\, a
 nd has since served mainly in the areas of China affairs and International
  Law. He most recently served as Director of the First China and Mongolia 
 Division (2024-25) and as Director of the International Legal Affairs Divi
 sion (2021-24).  He has also worked in Japan-US affairs\, in both economic
  and security matters\, serving in the North American Affairs Bureau (2008
 -10\, 2012-13). Additionally\, while serving as Deputy Director in the Nat
 ional Security Secretariat (2014)\, he worked on legal issues in Japanese 
 security policy regarding the Japanese Constitution in the context of the 
 Right of Self-Defense.  He has served as Executive Assistant to the Minist
 er for Foreign Affairs (2010-12) and to the Vice-Minister for Foreign Affa
 irs (2018-21). He has also served abroad in China as Counsellor (Political
  Section) of the Embassy of Japan in the People's Republic of China (2014-
 17).Prof. Adam LIFF (host/moderator) is Professor of East Asian Internatio
 nal Relations at Indiana University's Hamilton Lugar School of Global and 
 International Studies\, where he also serves as Founding Director of its 2
 1st Century Japan Politics and Society Initiative ("21JPSI"). Beyond IU\, 
 he is a Nonresident Senior Fellow with the Center for Asia Policy Studies 
 at the Brookings Institution\, as well as a Faculty Associate and Associat
 e-in-Research at Harvard University's Program on U.S.-Japan Relations and 
 Reischauer Institute of Japanese Studies. His research examines contempora
 ry international security and foreign policy challenges in East Asia—wit
 h a particular focus on Japanese politics and foreign policy\; the U.S.-Ja
 pan security alliance\; U.S. allies and Taiwan\; U.S. Asia-Pacific strateg
 y\; and U.S.-Japan-China relations. He holds a Ph.D. and M.A. in Politics 
 from Princeton University\, a postgraduate research certificate in Interna
 tional Politics from the University of Tokyo\, and a B.A. in East Asian St
 udies and Psychology from Stanford University.  &#160\;Unless otherwise no
 ted\, all 21JPSI events are 100% in-person.
GEO:39.170002;-86.517492
LAST-MODIFIED:20260925T144645Z
LOCATION:GLOBAL &amp\; INTERNATIONAL STUDIES BUILDING
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2429414-date-change-21jpsi-special-e
 vent-the-us-japan-allian
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><em>Minister Odaira's prepared remarks wil
 l be followed by a Q&amp\;A/discussion moderated by 21JPSI Director Adam P
 . Liff. <strong>**Please note that this event will be held under <a href="
 https://www.chathamhouse.org/about-us/chatham-house-rule" target="_blank" 
 rel="noopener">the Chatham House rule (no recording\, attribution\, or quo
 ting\, please).</a> .**&#160\;</strong></em></p><p>  In his first public t
 alk on the subject\, <strong>Minister for Public Affairs Masatsugu Odaira<
 /strong> (Embassy of Japan in Washington\, DC) will provide his personal a
 ssessment of the current and future of U.S.-Japan relations and discuss Ja
 pan's Free and Open Indo-Pacific (FOIP) vision–which Prime Minister Taka
 ichi updated in a major speech in Vietnam this past spring. Against the ba
 ckdrop of "an increasingly severe international environment" and intensify
 ing geopolitical competition\, Japan's updated FOIP framework expands Toky
 o's vision of maritime security\, digital connectivity\, and economic resi
 lience. It is designed to foster multi-ocean collaboration between East As
 ia\, ASEAN\, South Asia\, the Pacific\, and coastal Africa to ensure regio
 nal stability.&#160\;</p><p><em>This event is open to all students\, facul
 ty\, staff\, and members of the community.&#160\;</em></p><p><strong><span
  style="text-decoration: underline\;">About the speaker:</span></strong></
 p><p><strong>Mr. Masatsugu ODAIRA\,</strong> Minister of Public Affairs at
  the Embassy of Japan in the United States of America\, assumed his presen
 t position in September 2025.</p><p>  Minister Odaira joined the Ministry 
 of Foreign Affairs in 1998 after graduating from Tokyo University's Facult
 y of Law\, and has since served mainly in the areas of China affairs and I
 nternational Law. He most recently served as Director of the First China a
 nd Mongolia Division (2024-25) and as Director of the International Legal 
 Affairs Division (2021-24).</p><p>  He has also worked in Japan-US affairs
 \, in both economic and security matters\, serving in the North American A
 ffairs Bureau (2008-10\, 2012-13). Additionally\, while serving as Deputy 
 Director in the National Security Secretariat (2014)\, he worked on legal 
 issues in Japanese security policy regarding the Japanese Constitution in 
 the context of the Right of Self-Defense.</p><p>  He has served as Executi
 ve Assistant to the Minister for Foreign Affairs (2010-12) and to the Vice
 -Minister for Foreign Affairs (2018-21). He has also served abroad in Chin
 a as Counsellor (Political Section) of the Embassy of Japan in the People'
 s Republic of China (2014-17).</p><p><strong>Prof. Adam LIFF (host/moderat
 or)</strong> is Professor of East Asian International Relations at Indiana
  University's Hamilton Lugar School of Global and International Studies\, 
 where he also serves as Founding Director of its 21st Century Japan Politi
 cs and Society Initiative ("21JPSI"). Beyond IU\, he is a Nonresident Seni
 or Fellow with the Center for Asia Policy Studies at the Brookings Institu
 tion\, as well as a Faculty Associate and Associate-in-Research at Harvard
  University's Program on U.S.-Japan Relations and Reischauer Institute of 
 Japanese Studies. His research examines contemporary international securit
 y and foreign policy challenges in East Asia—with a particular focus on 
 Japanese politics and foreign policy\; the U.S.-Japan security alliance\; 
 U.S. allies and Taiwan\; U.S. Asia-Pacific strategy\; and U.S.-Japan-China
  relations. He holds a Ph.D. and M.A. in Politics from Princeton Universit
 y\, a postgraduate research certificate in International Politics from the
  University of Tokyo\, and a B.A. in East Asian Studies and Psychology fro
 m Stanford University.</p><p>  &#160\;</p><p><em>Unless otherwise noted\, 
 all 21JPSI events are 100% in-person.</em></p>
