BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 135": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2398640-lifeline-the-story-of-pepfar-fireside-chat"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_135
SUMMARY:Lifeline: The Story of PEPFAR Fireside Chat
DTSTART:20261020T220000Z
DTEND:20261021T000000Z
DTSTAMP:20260831T153520Z
UID:20261020T220000Z-2402253@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261020T220000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/87/width/168/height/168/crop/1
 /src_region/0,0,250,250/32228_lifeline_square_250.rev.1787846332.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Join the Indiana University Center for Global Health (https://
 globalhealth.iu.edu/) for a Fireside Chat with global health leaders and a
 uthor John Donnelly. Donnelly will discuss his new book "Lifeline: The Sto
 ry of PEPFAR\, the Greatest Humanitarian Initiative of Our Time" (https://
 www.johndonnellyauthor.com/) .  Hoosiers played a key role in the establis
 hment and success of PEPFAR. Ambassador Randy Tobias\, first U.S. Global A
 IDS Coordinator\; IU Center for Global Health director Dr. Adrian Gardner\
 ; and leaders from AMPATH Kenya's HIV program\, one of the first and most 
 successful PEPFAR partners\, will be in attendance to add to the conversat
 ion.  When President George W. Bush launched the President's Emergency Pla
 n for AIDS Relief (PEPFAR) in 2003 in response to the AIDS crisis devastat
 ing sub-Saharan Africa\, millions of people were dying every year and enti
 re nations were being destabilized by the pandemic. Over the next two deca
 des\, PEPFAR saved more than 26 million lives\, prevented nearly 8 million
  infant HIV infections\, strengthened health systems\, and helped bolster 
 economies and governments across Africa.  Drawing on a wealth of internal 
 documents and interviews with more than 200 participants\, Lifeline reveal
 s how an unlikely coalition of leaders and advocates overcame political\, 
 logistical\, and cultural obstacles to build a program many thought was im
 possible. John Donnelly is uniquely positioned to chronicle this history. 
 As a reporter for The Boston Globe\, he spent more than a decade covering 
 AIDS policy and America's response to the epidemic\, living in South Afric
 a and reporting across the continent during PEPFAR's formative years.  Att
 endees can pre-order a signed copy of John Donnelly's new book "Lifeline" 
 to pick up at the event. Please visit Golden Hour Books (https://www.golde
 nhourbooks.net/item/1sM1NJwkMQeztDkePKtpUQ) website to order and indicate 
 that you will pick up at the event.  Light snacks will be served.
GEO:39.77848;-86.144393
LAST-MODIFIED:20260831T193520Z
LOCATION:High Alpha
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2398640-lifeline-the-story-of-pepfar
 -fireside-chat
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Join the <a title="https://globalhealth.
 iu.edu/" href="https://globalhealth.iu.edu/" target="_blank" rel="nofollow
  noopener noreferrer" data-msys-clicktrack="0">Indiana University Center f
 or Global Health</a> for a Fireside Chat with global health leaders and au
 thor John Donnelly. Donnelly will discuss his new book <a title="https://w
 ww.johndonnellyauthor.com/" href="https://www.johndonnellyauthor.com/" tar
 get="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener noreferrer" data-msys-clicktrack="0">"
 Lifeline: The Story of PEPFAR\, the Greatest Humanitarian Initiative of Ou
 r Time"</a> .</p><p>  Hoosiers played a key role in the establishment and 
 success of PEPFAR. Ambassador Randy Tobias\, first U.S. Global AIDS Coordi
 nator\; IU Center for Global Health director Dr. Adrian Gardner\; and lead
 ers from AMPATH Kenya's HIV program\, one of the first and most successful
  PEPFAR partners\, will be in attendance to add to the conversation.</p><p
 >  When President George W. Bush launched the President's Emergency Plan f
 or AIDS Relief (PEPFAR) in 2003 in response to the AIDS crisis devastating
  sub-Saharan Africa\, millions of people were dying every year and entire 
 nations were being destabilized by the pandemic. Over the next two decades
 \, PEPFAR saved more than 26 million lives\, prevented nearly 8 million in
 fant HIV infections\, strengthened health systems\, and helped bolster eco
 nomies and governments across Africa.</p><p>  Drawing on a wealth of inter
 nal documents and interviews with more than 200 participants\, Lifeline re
 veals how an unlikely coalition of leaders and advocates overcame politica
 l\, logistical\, and cultural obstacles to build a program many thought wa
 s impossible. John Donnelly is uniquely positioned to chronicle this histo
 ry. As a reporter for <em>The Boston Globe</em>\, he spent more than a dec
 ade covering AIDS policy and America's response to the epidemic\, living i
 n South Africa and reporting across the continent during PEPFAR's formativ
 e years.</p><p>  Attendees can pre-order a signed copy of John Donnelly's 
 new book "Lifeline" to pick up at the event. Please visit <a title="https:
 //www.goldenhourbooks.net/item/1sM1NJwkMQeztDkePKtpUQ" href="https://www.g
 oldenhourbooks.net/item/1sM1NJwkMQeztDkePKtpUQ" target="_blank" rel="nofol
 low noopener noreferrer" data-msys-clicktrack="0">Golden Hour Books</a> we
 bsite to order and indicate that you will pick up at the event.</p><p>  Li
 ght snacks will be served.</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<p>\n  Kristin Srour<br />\n  ksrour@iu.edu\n</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-COST:Free
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2402253
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/87/width/168/height
 /168/crop/1/src_region/0\,0\,250\,250/32228_lifeline_square_250.rev.178784
 6332.jpg
X-LIVEWHALE-SUMMARY:<p>\n  Author John Donnelly &amp\; global leaders disc
 uss his new book Lifeline: The Story of PEPFAR\, the Greatest Humanitarian
  Initiative of Our Time.\n</p>
