X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 261": "https://events.iu.edu/libraries/event/2426680-keynote-reclaiming-the-commons-restructuring"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_261
SUMMARY:Keynote: Reclaiming the Commons: Restructuring Scholarly Communica
 tion with Digital Public Goods
DTSTART:20261021T180000Z
DTEND:20261021T190000Z
DTSTAMP:20260922T112112Z
UID:20261021T180000Z-2426680@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261021T180000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/261/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,3200,3200/32496_oa_week_2026_livewhale.rev.1789410307.jpg
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:TopicCommercial publishing models continue to gatekeep scholar
 ly outputs through high subscription costs and author fees. Drawing from L
 atin America's long-standing tradition of community-led openness\, coopera
 tion\, and non-commercial knowledge sharing\, this keynote will explore th
 e necessary transformation of scholarly communication. By positioning open
  access infrastructure and non-commercial publishing models as essential d
 igital public goods\, the session will reflect on regional models of knowl
 edge sovereignty and practical frameworks for building sustainable\, inter
 operable\, and ethically aligned open science infrastructures that return 
 control of the academic commons to the global scholarly community.Keynote 
 SpeakerArianna Becerril Garcíaholds a Ph.D. and M.Sc. in Computer Science
  from Tecnológico de Monterrey and a bachelor's degree in computer engine
 ering from Autonomous University of the State of Mexico (UAEMex). She rece
 ived the International Science Council's Early Career Scientist Award and 
 is a full-time professor and the Secretary of Science at UAEMex.She co-fou
 nded theRedalycopen infrastructure\, driving non-APC Diamond Open Access p
 ublishing across Latin America and now expanding worldwide. She also co-fo
 unded and leadsAmeliCAOpen Knowledge\, a community-driven initiative advoc
 ating non-commercial scholarly communication and regional knowledge sovere
 ignty.Becerril García has served on advisory and council boards for inter
 national organizations\, including the Directory of Open Access Journals (
 DOAJ) and the National System of Scientific Publications of Mexico. She co
 llaborates on UNESCO-supported open science projects globally and coordina
 tes a Latin American research group at the Latin American Council of Socia
 l Sciences (CLACSO).IntroductionAnita Say Chan\, Chair and Professor\, Dep
 artment of Information and Library Science\, Luddy School of Informatics\,
  Computing\, and Engineering\, IU Bloomington
LAST-MODIFIED:20260922T154756Z
URL:https://events.iu.edu/libraries/event/2426680-keynote-reclaiming-the-c
 ommons-restructuring
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p aria-level="2"><strong>Topic</strong><span
  data-ccp-props="{&quot\;134245418&quot\;:true\,&quot\;134245529&quot\;:tr
 ue\,&quot\;201341983&quot\;:0\,&quot\;335559738&quot\;:160\,&quot\;3355597
 39&quot\;:80\,&quot\;335559740&quot\;:279}"></span></p><p><span data-contr
 ast="auto">Commercial publishing models continue to gatekeep scholarly out
 puts through high subscription costs and author fees. Drawing from Latin A
 merica's long-standing tradition of community-led openness\, cooperation\,
  and non-commercial knowledge sharing\, this keynote will explore the nece
 ssary transformation of scholarly communication. By positioning open acces
 s infrastructure and non-commercial publishing models as essential digital
  public goods\, the session will reflect on regional models of knowledge s
 overeignty and practical frameworks for building sustainable\, interoperab
 le\, and ethically aligned open science infrastructures that return contro
 l of the academic commons to the global scholarly community.</span><span d
 ata-ccp-props="{&quot\;201341983&quot\;:0\,&quot\;335559740&quot\;:279}"><
 /span></p><p aria-level="2"><strong>Keynote Speaker</strong><span data-ccp
 -props="{&quot\;134245418&quot\;:true\,&quot\;134245529&quot\;:true\,&quot
 \;201341983&quot\;:0\,&quot\;335559738&quot\;:160\,&quot\;335559739&quot\;
 :80\,&quot\;335559740&quot\;:279}"></span></p><p><a href="https://ciencia.
 uaemex.mx/en/about-us/organizational-chart-research/"><span data-contrast=
 "none">Arianna Becerril García</span></a><span data-contrast="auto">holds
  a Ph.D. and M.Sc. in Computer Science from Tecnológico de Monterrey and 
 a bachelor's degree in computer engineering from Autonomous University of 
 the State of Mexico (UAEMex). She received the International Science Counc
 il's Early Career Scientist Award and is a full-time professor and the Sec
 retary of Science at UAEMex.</span><span data-ccp-props="{&quot\;201341983
 &quot\;:0\,&quot\;335559740&quot\;:279}"></span></p><p><span data-contrast
 ="auto">She co-founded the</span><a href="https://www.redalyc.org/"><span 
 data-contrast="none">Redalyc</span></a><span data-contrast="auto">open inf
 rastructure\, driving non-APC Diamond Open Access publishing across Latin 
 America and now expanding worldwide. She also co-founded and leads</span><
 a href="https://amelica.org/index.php/en/home/"><span data-contrast="none"
 >AmeliCA</span></a><span data-contrast="auto">Open Knowledge\, a community
 -driven initiative advocating non-commercial scholarly communication and r
 egional knowledge sovereignty.</span><span data-ccp-props="{&quot\;2013419
 83&quot\;:0\,&quot\;335559740&quot\;:279}"></span></p><p><span data-contra
 st="auto">Becerril García has served on advisory and council boards for i
 nternational organizations\, including the Directory of Open Access Journa
 ls (DOAJ) and the National System of Scientific Publications of Mexico. Sh
 e collaborates on UNESCO-supported open science projects globally and coor
 dinates a Latin American research group at the Latin American Council of S
