X-SOURCE-URLS:{"IU Bloomington Libraries": "https://events.iu.edu/libraries/event/2426618-the-cost-of-incentives-making-open-scholarship-the", "IU Kelley School of Business": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2426618-the-cost-of-incentives-making-open-scholarship-the"}
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 261": "https://events.iu.edu/libraries/event/2426618-the-cost-of-incentives-making-open-scholarship-the"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_261
SUMMARY:The Cost of Incentives: Making Open Scholarship the Norm
DTSTART:20261023T140000Z
DTEND:20261023T153000Z
DTSTAMP:20260922T110920Z
UID:20261023T140000Z-2426618@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261023T140000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/261/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,3200,3200/32496_oa_week_2026_livewhale.rev.1789410307.jpg
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:But making open scholarship the norm raises difficult question
 s about incentives\, equity\, and institutional responsibility. How can op
 en scholarship be encouraged without it becoming another unsupported expec
 tation\, particularly when researchers have unequal access to funding\, in
 frastructure\, time\, and disciplinary support? Should scholars be expecte
 d to adopt open practices\, and if so\, what responsibilities do universit
 ies\, publishers\, and other institutions have to make those practices fea
 sible?  This panel will therefore examine efforts to normalize open schola
 rship through changes to research assessment\, scholarly communication\, a
 nd research infrastructure. It will ask not only how researchers might be 
 encouraged to adopt open practices\, but also what universities\, publishe
 rs\, and other organizations must do to create the conditions that make op
 enness possible\, sustainable\, and professionally valued.PanelistsMichael
  Dougherty (https://psyc.umd.edu/facultyprofile/dougherty/michael)\, Chair
  &amp\; Professor\, Department of Psychology\, University of Maryland  Cai
 tlin Schleicher (https://www.orcaopen.org/team)\, Director\, Community &am
 p\; Partnerships\, Open Research Community Accelerator  Bodo Stern (https:
 //www.hhmi.org/about/senior-leadership/bodo-stern)\, Chief of Strategic In
 itiatives\, Howard Hughes Medical Institute  ModeratorScott Shackelford (h
 ttps://kelley.iu.edu/faculty-research/faculty-directory/profile.html?id=sj
 shacke)\, Associate Vice President for Research\, Indiana University\; Vic
 e Chancellor for Research\, IU Bloomington\; Provost Professor of Business
  Law &amp\; Ethics\, IU Bloomington\; Director of the Program on Cybersecu
 rity and Internet Governance\, Ostrom Workshop
LAST-MODIFIED:20260922T154759Z
URL:https://events.iu.edu/libraries/event/2426618-the-cost-of-incentives-m
 aking-open-scholarship-the
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  But making open scholarship the norm rai
 ses difficult questions about incentives\, equity\, and institutional resp
 onsibility. How can open scholarship be encouraged without it becoming ano
 ther unsupported expectation\, particularly when researchers have unequal 
 access to funding\, infrastructure\, time\, and disciplinary support? Shou
 ld scholars be expected to adopt open practices\, and if so\, what respons
 ibilities do universities\, publishers\, and other institutions have to ma
 ke those practices feasible?</p><p>  This panel will therefore examine eff
 orts to normalize open scholarship through changes to research assessment\
 , scholarly communication\, and research infrastructure. It will ask not o
 nly how researchers might be encouraged to adopt open practices\, but also
  what universities\, publishers\, and other organizations must do to creat
 e the conditions that make openness possible\, sustainable\, and professio
 nally valued.</p><p><strong>Panelists</strong></p><ul><li><a href="https:/
 /psyc.umd.edu/facultyprofile/dougherty/michael">Michael Dougherty</a>\, Ch
 air &amp\; Professor\, Department of Psychology\, University of Maryland  
 </li><li><a href="https://www.orcaopen.org/team">Caitlin Schleicher</a>\, 
 Director\, Community &amp\; Partnerships\, Open Research Community Acceler
 ator  </li><li><a href="https://www.hhmi.org/about/senior-leadership/bodo-
 stern">Bodo Stern</a>\, Chief of Strategic Initiatives\, Howard Hughes Med
 ical Institute  </li></ul><p><strong>Moderator</strong></p><ul><li><a href
 ="https://kelley.iu.edu/faculty-research/faculty-directory/profile.html?id
 =sjshacke">Scott Shackelford</a>\, Associate Vice President for Research\,
  Indiana University\; Vice Chancellor for Research\, IU Bloomington\; Prov
 ost Professor of Business Law &amp\; Ethics\, IU Bloomington\; Director of
  the Program on Cybersecurity and Internet Governance\, Ostrom Workshop  <
 /li></ul>
X-LIVEWHALE-COST:Free
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2426618
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/261/width/168/heigh
 t/168/crop/1/src_region/0\,0\,3200\,3200/32496_oa_week_2026_livewhale.rev.
 1789410307.jpg
X-LIVEWHALE-IS-ONLINE:1
X-LIVEWHALE-ONLINE-INSTRUCTIONS:<p>\n  You will receive the Zoom link upon
  registration.&#160\;\n</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-ONLINE-TYPE:Online only
X-LIVEWHALE-RSVP:1
X-LIVEWHALE-SUMMARY:<p>\n  Funders\, governments\, and universities are in
 creasingly seeking to make open scholarship a routine and valued part of a
 cademic practice.&#160\;\n</p>
