X-SOURCE-URLS:{"IU Arts & Humanities Institute": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2383524-master-class-in-documentary-filmmaking-with", "IU Media School": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2383524-master-class-in-documentary-filmmaking-with"}
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 130": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2383524-master-class-in-documentary-filmmaking-with"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_130
SUMMARY:Master Class in Documentary Filmmaking with Natalie Pattillo
DTSTART:20261023T173000Z
DTEND:20261023T181500Z
DTSTAMP:20260914T121651Z
UID:20261023T173000Z-2417209@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261023T173000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/199/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,3200,2133/32011_headshotNP_1.rev.1786914952.jpg
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences,Training, non-credit
  classes, and learning opportunities
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, October 231:30 - 2:15 PM  In-Person\, Lindley Hall 32
 8  In recognition of Domestic Violence Awareness Month\, join award-winnin
 g documentary filmmaker Natalie Pattillo for a master class in documentary
  filmmaking.  This practical and interactive conversation will focus on do
 cumentary filmmaking\, ethical storytelling\, working with vulnerable subj
 ects\, survivor-centered narratives\, and the responsibilities of filmmake
 rs telling stories involving trauma and violence.Participants will come aw
 ay with tools for:Story Development: Finding and developing a story with s
 takes\, tension\, and emotion while staying grounded in your project's "No
 rth Star."  Anatomy of a Scene &amp\; Thinking Visually: Unpacking the cho
 ices behind creating impactful scenes and adapting when things don't go as
  planned.  Interviewing\, Trust\, and Connection: Building trust with film
  participants\, asking thoughtful questions\, listening closely\, and maki
 ng space for complexity or unexpected threads.  Ethics\, Boundaries\, and 
 Care: Navigating consent\, safety\, power\, responsibility\, sensitive top
 ics\, and everyone on set's well-being (including your own!).  Creative Co
 llaboration: Working with a co-director\, cinematographers\, editor\, soun
 d team\, and music composer to create a cohesive film and being okay with 
 learning on the fly.  Funding\, Distribution\, and Impact: Finding ways to
  fund the work (challenging but not impossible!)\, defining what success l
 ooks like for you/your film\, and getting a film to communities that will 
 use it as a tool for powerful impact.    —** You can also join Natalie P
 attillo on Friday\, October 23 at 2:30 PM for a Domestic Violence\, Storyt
 elling\, and Social Change Workshop (https://events.iu.edu/kinsey/event/23
 83527-domestic-violence-storytelling-and-social-change). Natalie's documen
 tary film\, And So I Stayed will screen at the IU Cinema on Saturday\, Oct
 ober 24 at 7:00 PM. **Natalie Pattillo is an award-winning documentary fil
 mmaker\, writer and journalist whose work explores education\, child care 
 access\, gender-based violence\, food and culture. Her work has appeared i
 n The New York Times\, MSNBC\, VICE\, New York Magazine\, Al Jazeera Ameri
 ca\, Salon and beyond. In 2020\, she received the Media Award from the Nat
 ional Coalition Against Domestic Violence. She was also named a Faculty Ne
 ws Champion by the Center for Community News at the University of Vermont 
 for her work supporting student journalism and rebuilding local news.  Nat
 alie was the co-director\, writer and producer of And So I Stayed (https:/
 /nam12.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https:%2F%2Fwww.nytimes.com%2
 F2021%2F06%2F11%2Fmovies%2Fdomestic-violence-law.html&amp\;data=05%7C02%7C
 mrolfes%40iu.edu%7C68533c4a975c402135b208def48f0787%7C1113be34aed14d00ab4b
 cdd02510be91%7C1%7C0%7C639217092379681366%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0
 eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIj
 oyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&amp\;sdata=%2Fi1Tl7bk%2F8Acy%2B5RDgCzQCc%2F7gSL9gR
 5STvkaDCSvoQ%3D&amp\;reserved=0)\, an award-winning documentary about surv
 ivors of domestic violence who were incarcerated for acts of survival. The
  film has been featured by The New York Times\, the Chicago Tribune and AB
 C News.  She is currently working on Writing for My Mother (https://nam12.
 safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https:%2F%2Fwww.documentaries.org%2F
 films%2Fwriting-for-my-mother%2F&amp\;data=05%7C02%7Cmrolfes%40iu.edu%7C68
 533c4a975c402135b208def48f0787%7C1113be34aed14d00ab4bcdd02510be91%7C1%7C0%
 7C639217092379713731%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiO
 iIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%
 7C&amp\;sdata=HJDUrVkh4JRuRMGml0LyUARQNxyWimVxZrLDyPnKf38%3D&amp\;reserved
 =0)\, a documentary and book project exploring literacy in the United Stat
 es\, the communities that have been excluded from educational opportunitie
 s and what becomes possible in people's lives and for our democracy when t
 hose barriers are broken down.  Natalie lives in Providence\, Rhode Island
 \, with her husband and three children. In her free time\, she can usually
  be found gardening\, knitting\, reading\, or taking unhurried walks.AND S
 O I STAYEDis an&#160\;eye-opening and award-winning documentary about thre
 e survivors of abuse fighting for their lives and how the legal system bet
 rayed them.&#160\;These women paid a steep price with long prison sentence
 s\, lost time with loved ones\, and painful memories. Formerly incarcerate
 d survivor-advocate Kim Dadou Brown\, who met her wife while incarcerated\
 , is a driving force in the passage of New York's Domestic Violence Surviv
 ors Justice Act (DVSJA)\, a new law meant to prevent survivors from receiv
 ing harsh prison sentences for their acts of survival. Nikki Addimando\, a
  mother of two young children\, suffered the consequences when a judge did
 n't follow the law's guidelines. Tanisha Davis\, a single mother who was r
 ipped away from her son in 2013\, is hopeful the new law is her way out of
  a harsh prison sentence.AND SO I STAYED aims to hold the legal system and
  public accountable by exposing the unjust misogyny and racism embedded in
  these power structures while also portraying the resilience\, hopes\, and
  dreams of survivors and their loved ones.
LAST-MODIFIED:20260915T163928Z
