 filmmaking-with
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<h5 style="text-align: center\;"><strong>Frid
 ay\, October 23</strong></h5><h5 style="text-align: center\;"><strong>1:30
  - 2:15 PM</strong></h5><p style="text-align: center\;">  In-Person\, Lind
 ley Hall 328</p><p>  In recognition of Domestic Violence Awareness Month\,
  join award-winning documentary filmmaker Natalie Pattillo for a master cl
 ass in documentary filmmaking.</p><p>  This practical and interactive conv
 ersation will focus on documentary filmmaking\, ethical storytelling\, wor
 king with vulnerable subjects\, survivor-centered narratives\, and the res
 ponsibilities of filmmakers telling stories involving trauma and violence.
 </p><p dir="ltr"><span data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody"><em><strong>Part
 icipants will come away with tools for</strong></em>:</span></p><ul><li di
 r="ltr"><strong>Story Development</strong>: Finding and developing a story
  with stakes\, tension\, and emotion while staying grounded in your projec
 t's "North Star."  </li><li dir="ltr"><strong>Anatomy of a Scene &amp\; Th
 inking Visually</strong>: Unpacking the choices behind creating impactful 
 scenes and adapting when things don't go as planned.  </li><li dir="ltr"><
 strong>Interviewing\, Trust\, and Connection</strong>: Building trust with
  film participants\, asking thoughtful questions\, listening closely\, and
  making space for complexity or unexpected threads.  </li><li dir="ltr"><s
 trong>Ethics\, Boundaries\, and Care</strong>: Navigating consent\, safety
 \, power\, responsibility\, sensitive topics\, and everyone on set's well-
 being (including your own!).  </li><li dir="ltr"><strong>Creative Collabor
 ation</strong>: Working with a co-director\, cinematographers\, editor\, s
 ound team\, and music composer to create a cohesive film and being okay wi
 th learning on the fly.  </li><li dir="ltr"><strong>Funding\, Distribution
 \, and Impact</strong>: Finding ways to fund the work (challenging but not
  impossible!)\, defining what success looks like for you/your film\, and g
 etting a film to communities that will use it as a tool for powerful impac
 t.  </li></ul><p style="text-align: center\;">  —</p><p style="text-alig
 n: center\;"><em>**</em> You can also join Natalie Pattillo on Friday\, Oc
 tober 23 at 2:30 PM for a <a href="https://events.iu.edu/kinsey/event/2383
 527-domestic-violence-storytelling-and-social-change" target="_blank" rel=
 "noopener">Domestic Violence\, Storytelling\, and Social Change Workshop</
 a>. Natalie's documentary film\, <a href="https://events.iu.edu/kinsey/eve
 nt/2383529-screening-of-and-so-i-stayed-with-filmmaker" target="_blank" re
 l="noopener"><em>And So I Stayed</em></a> will screen at the IU Cinema on 
 Saturday\, October 24 at 7:00 PM. **</p><p><span data-olk-copy-source="Mes
 sageBody"><strong>Natalie Pattillo</strong> is an award-winning documentar
 y filmmaker\, writer and journalist whose work explores education\, child 
 care access\, gender-based violence\, food and culture. Her work has appea
 red in The New York Times\, MSNBC\, VICE\, New York Magazine\, Al Jazeera 
 America\, Salon and beyond. In 2020\, she received the Media Award from th
 e National Coalition Against Domestic Violence. She was also named a Facul
 ty News Champion by the Center for Community News at the University of Ver
 mont for her work supporting student journalism and rebuilding local news.
 </span></p><p>  Natalie was the co-director\, writer and producer of <em><
 a title="Original URL: https://www.nytimes.com/2021/06/11/movies/domestic-
 violence-law.html. Click or tap if you trust this link." href="https://nam
 12.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https:%2F%2Fwww.nytimes.com%2F202
 1%2F06%2F11%2Fmovies%2Fdomestic-violence-law.html&amp\;data=05%7C02%7Cmrol
 fes%40iu.edu%7C68533c4a975c402135b208def48f0787%7C1113be34aed14d00ab4bcdd0
 2510be91%7C1%7C0%7C639217092379681366%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1h
