 p\;data=05%7C02%7Cmrolfes%40iu.edu%7C68533c4a975c402135b208def48f0787%7C11
 13be34aed14d00ab4bcdd02510be91%7C1%7C0%7C639217092379713731%7CUnknown%7CTW
 FpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFO
 IjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&amp\;sdata=HJDUrVkh4JRuRMGml0LyU
 ARQNxyWimVxZrLDyPnKf38%3D&amp\;reserved=0" target="_blank" rel="noopener n
 oreferrer" data-auth="NotApplicable" data-linkindex="1">Writing for My Mot
 her</a></em>\, a documentary and book project exploring literacy in the Un
 ited States\, the communities that have been excluded from educational opp
 ortunities and what becomes possible in people's lives and for our democra
 cy when those barriers are broken down.</p><p>  Natalie lives in Providenc
 e\, Rhode Island\, with her husband and three children. In her free time\,
  she can usually be found gardening\, knitting\, reading\, or taking unhur
 ried walks.</p><p class="p1"><span class="s1"><a href="https://andsoistaye
 dfilm.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><strong><picture class="lw_imag
