X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_130
SUMMARY:Domestic Violence\, Storytelling\, and Social Change Workshop with
  Filmmaker Natalie Pattillo
DTSTART:20261023T183000Z
DTEND:20261023T193000Z
DTSTAMP:20260914T121652Z
UID:20261023T183000Z-2417212@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261023T183000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/199/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,3200,2133/32011_headshotNP_1.rev.1786914952.jpg
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:Friday\, October 232:30 - 3:30 PM  In-Person\, Lindley Hall 10
 2  In recognition of Domestic Violence Awareness Month\, join award-winnin
 g documentary filmmaker Natalie Pattillo for a powerful discussion on dome
 stic violence.  The conversation will explore the criminalization of survi
 vors\, the role of institutions and public policy\, and how communication 
 and storytelling can raise awareness\, shape public opinion\, and drive so
 cial change.Participants will explore and engage in powerful discussions a
 bout:Why Storytelling Matters: How stories can make complex systems easier
  to understand\, build empathy\, shift public perception\, and help people
  imagine better solutions.  Building Community Around Survivors: How frien
 ds\, families\, advocates\, workplaces\, legal teams\, service providers\,
  and institutions can support survivors and help them feel less isolated. 
  Seeing the Whole System: How housing\, employment\, food access\, child c
 are\, education\, health care\, law\, and public policy can impact survivo
 rs\, and how meaningful change will require us looking across systems toge
 ther.  Collaboration and Ethics: Drawing from documentary practice and tra
 uma-informed guidance to explore how people across fields can create envir
 onments that prioritize survivors' safety\, agency\, trust\, and choice wh
 ile navigating consent\, boundaries\, power\, and confidentiality.    —*
 * You can also join Natalie Pattillo on Friday\, October 23 at 1:30 PM for
  a Master Class in Documentary Filmmaking (https://events.iu.edu/kinsey/ev
 ent/2383524-master-class-in-documentary-filmmaking-with).  Natalie's docum
 entary film\, And So I Stayed will screen at the IU Cinema on Saturday\, O
 ctober 24 at 7:00 PM. **Natalie Pattillo is an award-winning documentary f
 ilmmaker\, writer and journalist whose work explores education\, child car
 e access\, gender-based violence\, food and culture. Her work has appeared
  in The New York Times\, MSNBC\, VICE\, New York Magazine\, Al Jazeera Ame
 rica\, Salon and beyond. In 2020\, she received the Media Award from the N
 ational Coalition Against Domestic Violence. She was also named a Faculty 
 News Champion by the Center for Community News at the University of Vermon
 t for her work supporting student journalism and rebuilding local news.  N
 atalie was the co-director\, writer and producer of And So I Stayed (https
 ://nam12.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https:%2F%2Fwww.nytimes.com
 %2F2021%2F06%2F11%2Fmovies%2Fdomestic-violence-law.html&amp\;data=05%7C02%
 7Cmrolfes%40iu.edu%7C68533c4a975c402135b208def48f0787%7C1113be34aed14d00ab
 4bcdd02510be91%7C1%7C0%7C639217092379681366%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbX
 B0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlYiOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldU
 IjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7C%7C&amp\;sdata=%2Fi1Tl7bk%2F8Acy%2B5RDgCzQCc%2F7gSL9
 gR5STvkaDCSvoQ%3D&amp\;reserved=0)\, an award-winning documentary about su
 rvivors of domestic violence who were incarcerated for acts of survival. T
 he film has been featured by The New York Times\, the Chicago Tribune and 
 ABC News.  She is currently working on Writing for My Mother (https://nam1
 2.safelinks.protection.outlook.com/?url=https:%2F%2Fwww.documentaries.org%
 2Ffilms%2Fwriting-for-my-mother%2F&amp\;data=05%7C02%7Cmrolfes%40iu.edu%7C
 68533c4a975c402135b208def48f0787%7C1113be34aed14d00ab4bcdd02510be91%7C1%7C
 0%7C639217092379713731%7CUnknown%7CTWFpbGZsb3d8eyJFbXB0eU1hcGkiOnRydWUsIlY
 iOiIwLjAuMDAwMCIsIlAiOiJXaW4zMiIsIkFOIjoiTWFpbCIsIldUIjoyfQ%3D%3D%7C0%7C%7
 C%7C&amp\;sdata=HJDUrVkh4JRuRMGml0LyUARQNxyWimVxZrLDyPnKf38%3D&amp\;reserv
 ed=0)\, a documentary and book project exploring literacy in the United St
 ates\, the communities that have been excluded from educational opportunit
 ies and what becomes possible in people's lives and for our democracy when
  those barriers are broken down.  Natalie lives in Providence\, Rhode Isla
 nd\, with her husband and three children. In her free time\, she can usual
 ly be found gardening\, knitting\, reading\, or taking unhurried walks.AND
  SO I STAYEDis an eye-opening and award-winning documentary about three su
 rvivors of abuse fighting for their lives and how the legal system betraye
 d them.These women paid a steep price with long prison sentences\, lost ti
 me with loved ones\, and painful memories. Formerly incarcerated survivor-
 advocate Kim Dadou Brown\, who met her wife while incarcerated\, is a driv
 ing force in the passage of New York's Domestic Violence Survivors Justice
  Act (DVSJA)\, a new law meant to prevent survivors from receiving harsh p
 rison sentences for their acts of survival. Nikki Addimando\, a mother of 
 two young children\, suffered the consequences when a judge didn't follow 
 the law's guidelines. Tanisha Davis\, a single mother who was ripped away 
 from her son in 2013\, is hopeful the new law is her way out of a harsh pr
 ison sentence.AND SO I STAYED aims to hold the legal system and public acc
 ountable by exposing the unjust misogyny and racism embedded in these powe
 r structures while also portraying the resilience\, hopes\, and dreams of 
 survivors and their loved ones.
LAST-MODIFIED:20260923T201351Z
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2383527-domestic-violence-storytelli
 ng-and-social-change
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<h5 style="text-align: center\;"><strong>Frid
 ay\, October 23</strong></h5><h5 style="text-align: center\;"><strong>2:30
  - 3:30 PM</strong></h5><p style="text-align: center\;">  In-Person\, Lind
 ley Hall 102</p><p>  In recognition of Domestic Violence Awareness Month\,
  join award-winning documentary filmmaker Natalie Pattillo for a powerful 
 discussion on domestic violence.</p><p class="font-claude-response-body br
 eak-words whitespace-normal" dir="ltr">  The conversation will explore the
  criminalization of survivors\, the role of institutions and public policy
 \, and how communication and storytelling can raise awareness\, shape publ
 ic opinion\, and drive social change.</p><p><em><strong>Participants will 
 explore and engage in powerful discussions about:</strong></em></p><ul><li
 ><strong>Why Storytelling Matters:</strong> How stories can make complex s
 ystems easier to understand\, build empathy\, shift public perception\, an
 d help people imagine better solutions.  </li><li><strong>Building Communi
 ty Around Survivors:</strong> How friends\, families\, advocates\, workpla
 ces\, legal teams\, service providers\, and institutions can support survi
 vors and help them feel less isolated.  </li><li><strong>Seeing the Whole 
 System:</strong> How housing\, employment\, food access\, child care\, edu
 cation\, health care\, law\, and public policy can impact survivors\, and 
 how meaningful change will require us looking across systems together.  </
 li><li><strong>Collaboration and Ethics:</strong> Drawing from documentary
  practice and trauma-informed guidance to explore how people across fields
  can create environments that prioritize survivors' safety\, agency\, trus
 t\, and choice while navigating consent\, boundaries\, power\, and confide
 ntiality.  </li></ul><p class="font-claude-response-body break-words white
 space-normal" dir="ltr" style="text-align: center\;">  —</p><p style="te
 xt-align: center\;"><em>**</em> You can also join Natalie Pattillo on Frid
