X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 261": "https://events.iu.edu/libraries/event/2405054-you-probably-havent-seen-this-before-american-parasi"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_261
SUMMARY:You (probably) Haven't Seen This Before - American Parasites
DTSTART:20261029T213000Z
DTEND:20261029T223000Z
DTSTAMP:20260902T132554Z
UID:20261029T213000Z-2405054@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261029T213000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/261/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,1536,2048/32330_Oct2026Sterling_headshot.rev.1788370047.p
 ng
CATEGORIES:Performances, shows, and films
DESCRIPTION:Dive deep into the archival collections of IU Libraries throug
 h a screening series of rare and unique archival films that center traditi
 onally underrepresented narratives and underrepresented motion-picture pra
 ctices\, pushing on and expanding the spectrum of what is considered "cine
 ma."  In an effort to keep audiences open-minded and with limited pre-conc
 eptions of these rare works of celluloid\, descriptions of films to be sho
 wn will not be shared prior to screenings. Join us based on announced them
 es and the expectation that the works you see will fall outside of Hollywo
 od tropes\, outside of mainstream commercial expectations\, and outside of
  conventional filmmaking practices. Trust us\, you (probably) haven't seen
  these films before!October Program: American Parasites curated by Andrea 
 M. SterlingProgram Description: This program invites the viewer to conside
 r the practice of parasitism\; a primary mechanism influencing structural 
 antagonisms that have undergirded race relations in America from the past 
 through the present. The two short films encourage the audience to questio
 n their current understandings of race relations\, the exploitation of Bla
 ck labor\, and the ways in which that labor has acted as invisible foundat
 ions in the practice of White cultural production.Curator Bio: Andrea M. S
 terling is a Bloomington transplant by way of Providence\, Rhode Island\, 
 originally moving here for graduate school and eventually planting roots. 
 Facilitation is in her heart\, body\, and soul—whether that's in a tradi
 tional classroom\, a poetry night in a dimly lit bar\, or contracted works
 hops across the country as a DEI/Transformative Justice consultant. She ha
 s co-organized the Bloomington Poetry Slam for the last 9 years\, facilita
 tes writing circles with Women Writing for a Change\, and mixes up tasty e
 lixirs at the Uptown Café.  As a graduate student in African-American and
  African Diaspora Studies\, Andrea's focus centered around constructions o
 f race relations in America\, the ideological mechanics of White Supremacy
  and Anti-Blackness\, and the power of a radical love ethic in building st
 rong communities and liberated futures. She continues to carry these theme
 s into her creative and facilitation work today.Please register below! Adv
 ance registration is strongly encouraged and deeply appreciated!
GEO:39.171006;-86.5168
LAST-MODIFIED:20260902T173808Z
LOCATION:Wells Library Screening Room 048
URL:https://events.iu.edu/libraries/event/2405054-you-probably-havent-seen
 -this-before-american-parasi
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Dive deep into the archival collections 
 of IU Libraries through a screening series of rare and unique archival fil
 ms that center traditionally underrepresented narratives and underrepresen
 ted motion-picture practices\, pushing on and expanding the spectrum of wh
 at is considered "cinema."</p><p>  In an effort to keep audiences open-min
 ded and with limited pre-conceptions of these rare works of celluloid\, de
 scriptions of films to be shown will not be shared prior to screenings. Jo
 in us based on announced themes and the expectation that the works you see
  will fall outside of Hollywood tropes\, outside of mainstream commercial 
 expectations\, and outside of conventional filmmaking practices. Trust us\
 , you (probably) haven't seen these films before!</p><p><strong>October Pr
 ogram:</strong> American Parasites curated by Andrea M. Sterling</p><p><st
 rong>Program Description:</strong> This program invites the viewer to cons
 ider the practice of parasitism\; a primary mechanism influencing structur
 al antagonisms that have undergirded race relations in America from the pa
 st through the present. The two short films encourage the audience to ques
 tion their current understandings of race relations\, the exploitation of 
 Black labor\, and the ways in which that labor has acted as invisible foun
 dations in the practice of White cultural production.</p><p><strong>Curato
 r Bio:</strong> Andrea M. Sterling is a Bloomington transplant by way of P
 rovidence\, Rhode Island\, originally moving here for graduate school and 
 eventually planting roots. Facilitation is in her heart\, body\, and soul
 —whether that's in a traditional classroom\, a poetry night in a dimly l
 it bar\, or contracted workshops across the country as a DEI/Transformativ
 e Justice consultant. She has co-organized the Bloomington Poetry Slam for
  the last 9 years\, facilitates writing circles with Women Writing for a C
 hange\, and mixes up tasty elixirs at the Uptown Café.</p><p>  As a gradu
 ate student in African-American and African Diaspora Studies\, Andrea's fo
 cus centered around constructions of race relations in America\, the ideol
 ogical mechanics of White Supremacy and Anti-Blackness\, and the power of 
 a radical love ethic in building strong communities and liberated futures.
  She continues to carry these themes into her creative and facilitation wo
 rk today.</p><p><strong>Please register below! Advance registration is str
 ongly encouraged and deeply appreciated!</strong></p><p><br style="font-we
 ight: 400\;" /><br style="font-weight: 400\;" /></p>
X-LIVEWHALE-COST:Free
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2405054
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/261/width/168/heigh
 t/168/crop/1/src_region/0\,0\,1536\,2048/32330_Oct2026Sterling_headshot.re
 v.1788370047.png
X-LIVEWHALE-RSVP:1
X-LIVEWHALE-SUMMARY:<p>\n  Join us for FREE monthly screenings of archival
  16mm film shorts you probably haven't seen before projected on our 1950's
  Kodak film projectors!\n</p>
