BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 130": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2388024-tobias-center-research-seminar-the-grids-rural-edge"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_130
SUMMARY:Tobias Center Research Seminar: The Grid's Rural Edge
DTSTART:20261030T160000Z
DTEND:20261030T173000Z
DTSTAMP:20260914T121708Z
UID:20261030T160000Z-2417265@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261030T160000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/185/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,1080,1350/32569_Research_Seminars_fall_26.rev.1789658339.
 png
DESCRIPTION:This installment of the Tobias Center research seminar series\
 , entitled "The grid's rural edge: Rethinking energy- and land-based clima
 te finance in upland Laos\," features Mike Dwyer\, Associate Professor in 
 IU's Department of Geography.Abstract:Climate finance is often separated c
 onceptually and operationally along lines that divide energy systems from 
 land use. At a moment of urgency in both societal efforts to address clima
 te change and specialist debates about climate finance\, this paper proble
 matizes the distinction between energy- and land-based climate finance by 
 examining how rural electricity access influences land and forest dynamics
  in the global South. Drawing on research in Laos\, the paper theorizes wh
 at I call the land-energy nexus by examining the conditions under which ru
 ral electrification can help improve farmer-controlled value adding\, and 
 thus improve the performance of transnational efforts to reduce deforestat
 ion and forest degradation (REDD+). In doing so\, it seeks to critically e
 xtend the concept of "land sparing"\, the theory of change that underlies 
 many REDD+ programs but has proven elusive in practice. The paper proposes
  two intersecting scales for understanding the land-energy nexus in the gl
 obal South: first\, a system- or large-landscape scale where land-tenure s
 ecurity\, labor mobility and electricity financing each bear on forest-cha
 nge dynamics\; and second\, a more localized landscape-to-village scale wh
 ere incentives to invest in value-adding – a process traditionally cente
 red in urban areas – are created (or eroded) in locations closer to the 
 farm-forest edge. The paper thus seeks to build theory about effective cli
 mate finance from the ground up\, drawing on landscapes where "actually ex
 isting REDD+" and recent rural electrification are overlapping in space an
 d time\, but not yet sufficiently in practice.  Lunch will be provided.
GEO:39.170002;-86.517492
LAST-MODIFIED:20260917T151915Z
LOCATION:GLOBAL &amp\; INTERNATIONAL STUDIES BUILDING
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2388024-tobias-center-research-semin
 ar-the-grids-rural-edge
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  This installment of the Tobias Center re
 search seminar series\, entitled "<span data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody"
 ><em>The grid's rural edge: Rethinking energy- and land-based climate fina
 nce in upland Laos</em>\,"</span> features Mike Dwyer\, Associate Professo
 r in IU's Department of Geography.</p><p><strong>Abstract:</strong><span d
 ata-olk-copy-source="MessageBody">Climate finance is often separated conce
 ptually and operationally along lines that divide energy systems from land
  use. At a moment of urgency in both societal efforts to address climate c
 hange and specialist debates about climate finance\, this paper problemati
 zes the distinction between energy- and land-based climate finance by exam
 ining how rural electricity access influences land and forest dynamics in 
 the global South. Drawing on research in Laos\, the paper theorizes what I
  call the land-energy nexus by examining the conditions under which rural 
 electrification can help improve farmer-controlled value adding\, and thus
  improve the performance of transnational efforts to reduce deforestation 
 and forest degradation (REDD+). In doing so\, it seeks to critically exten
 d the concept of "land sparing"\, the theory of change that underlies many
  REDD+ programs but has proven elusive in practice. The paper proposes two
  intersecting scales for understanding the land-energy nexus in the global
  South: first\, a system- or large-landscape scale where land-tenure secur
 ity\, labor mobility and electricity financing each bear on forest-change 
 dynamics\; and second\, a more localized landscape-to-village scale where 
 incentives to invest in value-adding – a process traditionally centered 
 in urban areas – are created (or eroded) in locations closer to the farm
 -forest edge. The paper thus seeks to build theory about effective climate
  finance from the ground up\, drawing on landscapes where "actually existi
 ng REDD+" and recent rural electrification are overlapping in space and ti
 me\, but not yet sufficiently in practice.</span></p><p>  Lunch will be pr
 ovided.</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2417265
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/185/width/168/heigh
 t/168/crop/1/src_region/0\,0\,1080\,1350/32569_Research_Seminars_fall_26.r
 ev.1789658339.png
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE-CAPTION:Event Flyer
X-LIVEWHALE-RSVP:1
X-LIVEWHALE-TIMEZONE:America/New_York
X-LIVEWHALE-TYPE:events
X-SOURCE:IU Arts & Humanities Institute
END:VEVENT
